      <span class="eyebrow">CHEM 18 · Thermodynamics &amp; chemical equilibrium</span>
      <h1 id="h-map">Where the energy goes, and which way things run</h1>
      <p class="lede">Thermodynamics answers two questions. The first law keeps the books: energy moves between system and surroundings as heat and work, but the total never changes. The second law decides direction: a process runs on its own only if it increases the entropy of the universe. Gibbs free energy combines both into one number you can compute. Chemical equilibrium is where the two meet: ΔG° = −RT ln K tells you how far a reaction goes before it stops, and Le Châtelier tells you which way it moves when you disturb it.</p>
      <div class="row">
        <a class="btn primary" href="#discussion">Start the discussion</a>
        <a class="btn" href="#past">Try the past quiz items</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="card flow" aria-label="The three equations of this unit">
      <h4>Four equations carry the unit</h4>
      <div class="eq">ΔE = q + w</div>
      <p style="font-size:15px">First law. Energy bookkeeping for the system.</p>
      <div class="eq">ΔH = ΔE + Δn<sub>g</sub>RT</div>
      <p style="font-size:15px">Enthalpy: the heat you measure at constant pressure.</p>
      <div class="eq">ΔG = ΔH − TΔS</div>
      <p style="font-size:15px">Spontaneity: ΔG &lt; 0 means the process can run on its own at constant T and P.</p>
      <div class="eq">ΔG° = −RT ln K</div>
      <p style="font-size:15px">Equilibrium: free energy fixes how far a reaction goes.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="flow" aria-labelledby="h-cmap">
