<article class="topic" id="d-sys" aria-labelledby="t1">
  <header><span class="eyebrow">Lesson 1</span><h2 id="t1">Systems, surroundings, and state functions</h2></header>
  <p><b>Chemical thermodynamics</b> is the study of energy change, transformation and exchange. Before you can track energy, you have to decide what you are tracking it <i>for</i>.</p>
  <div class="wh">
    <div class="k">What</div>
    <div class="v">
      <ul class="tight">
        <li><b>System:</b> the part of the universe under investigation, separated from the rest by a real or imaginary boundary.</li>
        <li><b>Surroundings:</b> the rest of the universe. System + surroundings = universe.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="tbl"><table>
        <caption>Three types of system</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">Exchanges matter?</th><th scope="col">Exchanges energy?</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Open</th><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>An uncovered beaker of boiling water; your body</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Closed</th><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>A stoppered flask; a sealed piston</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Isolated</th><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>An ideal thermos or a sealed bomb calorimeter (approximately)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <ul class="tight">
        <li><b>Extensive properties</b> depend on the amount: moles, mass, volume, energy, enthalpy.</li>
        <li><b>Intensive properties</b> do not: density, temperature, melting point, boiling point, solubility.</li>
        <li><b>State functions</b> depend only on the initial and final states: P, V, T, E (or U), H, S, G.</li>
        <li><b>Path (nonstate) functions</b> depend on how the change happened: work (w) and heat (q).</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="k">How</div>
    <div class="v">
      <p>In the handout's example, Zn(s) and HCl(aq) react in an open flask. The <b>system</b> is the reacting mixture in the flask. The flask, the air and the thermometer are the <b>surroundings</b>. Because H<sub>2</sub> gas escapes and heat leaves, it is an <b>open system</b>. Stopper the flask and it becomes closed.</p>
      <p><b>The letter clue:</b> state functions are usually written with capitals (E, H, S, G, P, V, T); path functions with lowercase (q, w). So in ΔE = q + w, a state function (ΔE) equals the sum of two path functions.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="k">Why</div>
    <div class="v why">
      <p>The handout's mountain picture says it best: the change in altitude between the base and the summit (4004 m) is the same whether you take the winding road or the helicopter. That is a state function. The distance traveled (5662 m vs 9857 m) depends on the route. That is a path function.</p>
      <p>This matters because it lets you calculate ΔH or ΔE by <i>any</i> convenient route, including routes that never happen in real life (Hess's law, Born–Haber cycles). You can never do that with q or w alone: the same overall change can release different amounts of heat and work depending on how it is carried out. Only their sum, ΔE, is fixed.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="check"><h4>Check yourself</h4>
    <details><summary>Is the heat released when 2 mol of methane burns extensive or intensive?</summary><p>Extensive. Burning twice as much releases twice the heat. ΔH per mole (kJ/mol) is the intensive version.</p></details>
    <details><summary>A reaction runs inside a sealed, perfectly insulated steel container. What type of system?</summary><p>Isolated: the seal blocks matter and the insulation blocks heat.</p></details>
  </div>
  <p class="seeit"><span>See it</span><a href="#v-ed">Enthalpy diagram</a></p>
</article>

<!-- 2 -->
<article class="topic" id="d-first" aria-labelledby="t2">
  <header><span class="eyebrow">Lesson 2</span><h2 id="t2">The first law of thermodynamics</h2></header>
  <div class="wh">
    <div class="k">What</div>
    <div class="v">
      <p><b>Energy is neither created nor destroyed</b> in a chemical reaction. It only moves between system and surroundings. The energy a system holds is its <b>internal energy, E</b>: the sum of all kinetic and potential energies of its particles. You cannot measure E itself, only its change.</p>
      <div class="eq">ΔE = E<sub>final</sub> − E<sub>initial</sub> = q + w &nbsp;&nbsp; (equivalently q = ΔE + (−w))</div>
      <div class="tbl"><table>
        <caption>Sign convention used in your course (chemists' convention)</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Quantity</th><th scope="col">Positive (+)</th><th scope="col">Negative (−)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">q (heat)</th><td><span class="sign p">+</span> heat absorbed by the system</td><td><span class="sign n">−</span> heat released by the system</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">w (work)</th><td><span class="sign p">+</span> work done <b>on</b> the system</td><td><span class="sign n">−</span> work done <b>by</b> the system</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">ΔE</th><td><span class="sign p">+</span> system gains energy (endothermic)</td><td><span class="sign n">−</span> system loses energy (exothermic)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
    </div>
    <div class="k">How</div>
    <div class="v">
      <p>Translate each word into a sign before you touch numbers. "Receives," "absorbs," "is added" mean q &gt; 0. "Loses," "releases," "gives off" mean q &lt; 0. "Does work," "delivers work," "expands against the atmosphere" mean w &lt; 0. "Is compressed," "work is done on it" mean w &gt; 0.</p>
      <details class="worked" open><summary>Handout Question 1: a system receives 575 J of heat and delivers 875 J of work. Find ΔE.</summary><div>
        <p>q = +575 J (received). w = −875 J (delivered by the system).</p>
        <p>ΔE = 575 + (−875) = <b>−300 J</b>. The system gave out more energy as work than it took in as heat, so its internal energy fell.</p>
      </div></details>
      <details class="worked"><summary>Handout Question 2: expanding gases do 450 J of work on pistons and the engine loses 330 J as heat. Find ΔE in kcal.</summary><div>
        <p>System = the burning gasoline and its products. q = −330 J (lost). w = −450 J (done by the gases).</p>
        <p>ΔE = −330 + (−450) = −780 J. Convert: −780 J × (1 kJ/1000 J) × (1 kcal/4.184 kJ) = <b>−0.186 kcal</b>.</p>
      </div></details>
      <p><b>Two special cases from your handout:</b></p>
      <ul class="tight">
        <li><b>No work (w = 0):</b> ΔE = q. Ice melting in a glass absorbs heat, so q &gt; 0 and ΔE &gt; 0 (endothermic). Water freezing releases heat: q &lt; 0, ΔE &lt; 0 (exothermic).</li>
        <li><b>No heat (q = 0), an adiabatic process:</b> ΔE = w. When Zn + HCl makes H<sub>2</sub> that pushes a piston in an insulated tank, all the energy leaves as work and ΔE &lt; 0.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="k">Why</div>
    <div class="v why">
      <p>Because energy is conserved, whatever the system loses the surroundings must gain, exactly: ΔE<sub>system</sub> = −ΔE<sub>surroundings</sub>. The handout's twin bar graphs show this: when the system's bar grows, the surroundings' bar shrinks by the same amount. The signs are written from the system's point of view, which is why "work done by the system" is negative: energy left the system.</p>
      <p>Heat and work are the only two ways energy crosses the boundary of a closed system. Heat flows because of a temperature difference; work is energy transferred when something is moved by a force (like a piston). That is why their sum accounts for the entire change in E.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="seeit"><span>See it</span><a href="#v-chain">How it all connects</a></p>
</article>

