<article class="topic" id="d-work" aria-labelledby="t3">
  <header><span class="eyebrow">Lesson 3</span><h2 id="t3">Work and the four types of process</h2></header>
  <div class="wh">
    <div class="k">What</div>
    <div class="v">
      <p>Most chemical work is <b>pressure–volume (PΔV) work</b>: a gas pushing back the atmosphere or a piston as it expands.</p>
      <div class="eq">w = −P<sub>ext</sub>ΔV &nbsp;&nbsp; (irreversible, against a constant external pressure)</div>
      <p>P<sub>ext</sub> is the <b>outside</b> pressure, not the gas pressure inside. With P in atm and V in L, w comes out in L·atm; multiply by 101.3 J/L·atm (or by 8.314/0.08205) to get joules.</p>
      <div class="eq">w<sub>rev</sub> = −nRT ln(V<sub>2</sub>/V<sub>1</sub>) &nbsp;&nbsp; (reversible, isothermal, ideal gas)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="k">How</div>
    <div class="v">
      <div class="tbl"><table>
        <caption>Four ways a gas can change (ideal gas)</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Process</th><th scope="col">Held constant</th><th scope="col">Key results</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Isobaric</th><td>Pressure</td><td>q<sub>p</sub> = nC<sub>p</sub>ΔT = ΔH; w = −PΔV = −nRΔT</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Isochoric</th><td>Volume</td><td>w = 0, so q<sub>v</sub> = ΔE = nC<sub>v</sub>ΔT</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Isothermal</th><td>Temperature</td><td>ΔE = 0 and ΔH = 0 for an ideal gas, so q = −w</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Adiabatic</th><td>No heat flow (q = 0)</td><td>ΔE = w: expansion work is paid for entirely from internal energy, so the gas cools</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <details class="worked" open><summary>Handout example: 1 mol ideal gas at 2 atm expands from 10.0 L to 14.0 L. Work if (a) irreversible against 1 atm, (b) reversible?</summary><div>
        <p><b>(a)</b> w = −(1 atm)(14.0 − 10.0 L) = −4.0 L·atm × 101.3 J/L·atm = <b>−405.3 J</b>.</p>
        <p><b>(b)</b> T = PV/nR = (2 atm)(10.0 L)/[(1 mol)(0.08205)] = 243.8 K. w = −(1)(8.314)(243.8) ln(14.0/10.0) = <b>−682 J</b>.</p>
        <p>The reversible path does about 1.7 times more work for the same expansion.</p>
      </div></details>
      <p>From isobaric heating the handout derives C<sub>p</sub> = C<sub>v</sub> + R for an ideal gas: at constant pressure the heat must both raise the temperature (nC<sub>v</sub>ΔT) and pay for expansion work (nRΔT).</p>
    </div>
    <div class="k">Why</div>
    <div class="v why">
      <p><b>Why reversible work is the maximum.</b> In a reversible process P<sub>ext</sub> is kept equal to P<sub>gas</sub> at every instant, so the gas always pushes against the largest pressure it can still overcome. In an irreversible expansion it pushes against a smaller, fixed P<sub>ext</sub> and the "extra" push is wasted. On a P–V graph, work is the area under the path; the reversible curve encloses more area than the flat line at P<sub>ext</sub>. (Lab 2 in Remember draws this.)</p>
      <p><b>Why ΔE = 0 for an isothermal ideal gas.</b> An ideal gas has no intermolecular forces, so its internal energy is only the kinetic energy of its molecules, which depends only on T. Same T, same E. Any heat absorbed therefore leaves immediately as work.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</article>

<!-- 4 -->
<article class="topic" id="d-enth" aria-labelledby="t4">
  <header><span class="eyebrow">Lesson 4</span><h2 id="t4">Enthalpy, H</h2></header>
  <div class="wh">
    <div class="k">What</div>
    <div class="v">
      <p><b>Enthalpy</b> is the thermodynamic variable equal to the heat gained or lost in a constant-pressure process. Most reactions in open beakers, and in your body, happen at constant pressure, which is why ΔH is the energy change you see in tables.</p>
      <div class="eq block">ΔH = ΔE + Δ(PV) &nbsp;&nbsp; at constant P: ΔH = ΔE + PΔV = q<sub>p</sub><br>For gases: Δ(PV) = Δn<sub>g</sub>RT, so <b>ΔH = ΔE + Δn<sub>g</sub>RT</b></div>
      <p>Δn<sub>g</sub> = moles of gaseous products − moles of gaseous reactants. Solids and liquids are ignored because their volumes are tiny.</p>
      <ul class="tight"><li>ΔH &lt; 0: <b>exothermic</b>, heat released to the surroundings.</li><li>ΔH &gt; 0: <b>endothermic</b>, heat absorbed from the surroundings.</li></ul>
    </div>
    <div class="k">How</div>
    <div class="v">
      <ol class="tight">
        <li>Count Δn<sub>g</sub> from the balanced equation (gases only).</li>
        <li>Use R in units that match: 8.314 × 10<sup>−3</sup> kJ/mol·K for kJ, or 1.987 cal/mol·K for cal.</li>
        <li>ΔH = ΔE + Δn<sub>g</sub>RT. If Δn<sub>g</sub> = 0, then ΔH = ΔE.</li>
      </ol>
      <details class="worked" open><summary>Past quiz: B<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub>(g) + 3O<sub>2</sub>(g) → B<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>(s) + 3H<sub>2</sub>O(l), ΔE° = −2143.2 kJ. Find ΔH°.</summary><div>
        <p>Gases: reactants 1 + 3 = 4 mol; products 0 (solid and liquid). Δn<sub>g</sub> = 0 − 4 = −4.</p>
        <p>ΔH = −2143.2 kJ + (−4)(8.314 × 10<sup>−3</sup>)(298) = −2143.2 − 9.9 = <b>−2153.1 kJ</b>.</p>
      </div></details>
      <p>ΔH scales with the equation: double the coefficients, double ΔH. Reverse the reaction, flip the sign. Propane combustion releases 2043.0 kJ per mole of propane no matter what path it takes.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="k">Why</div>
    <div class="v why">
      <p>At constant pressure, some of the energy change goes into PΔV work instead of heat. Enthalpy adds the PV term back in, so that q<sub>p</sub> = ΔH exactly. That is why a coffee-cup calorimeter measures ΔH while a sealed bomb calorimeter (no volume change, w = 0) measures ΔE.</p>
      <p>When gas moles <b>decrease</b> (Δn<sub>g</sub> &lt; 0), the atmosphere does work on the shrinking system, so more heat comes out at constant pressure than at constant volume: ΔH is more negative than ΔE, as in the B<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub> problem. The correction is small (RT ≈ 2.5 kJ per mole of gas at 25 °C) but exams test the sign.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="seeit"><span>See it</span><a href="#v-ed">Where the heat goes</a></p>
</article>

