<article class="topic" id="d-cal" aria-labelledby="t5">
  <header><span class="eyebrow">Lesson 5</span><h2 id="t5">Calorimetry</h2></header>
  <div class="wh">
    <div class="k">What</div>
    <div class="v">
      <p>A <b>calorimeter</b> measures heat flow from the temperature change of something with known heat capacity.</p>
      <div class="eq block">q = C<sub>cal</sub>ΔT (calorimeter constant, kJ/°C) &nbsp;·&nbsp; q = m·c·ΔT (specific heat) &nbsp;·&nbsp; q = n·C<sub>m</sub>·ΔT (molar heat capacity)<br>q<sub>reaction</sub> = −q<sub>calorimeter</sub></div>
      <ul class="tight">
        <li><b>Bomb calorimeter</b>: sealed steel vessel, <b>constant volume</b>, w = 0, so q<sub>v</sub> = ΔE.</li>
        <li><b>Coffee-cup (constant-pressure) calorimeter</b>: open to the atmosphere, <b>constant pressure</b>, so q<sub>p</sub> = ΔH.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="k">How</div>
    <div class="v">
      <details class="worked" open><summary>Handout Worked Example 1: 10.3 mg benzoic acid (MW 122) burns in a bomb; T rises 24.5 → 25.1 °C; C<sub>cal</sub> = 0.22 kJ/°C. Find q<sub>v</sub>, ΔE, ΔH and q<sub>p</sub>.</summary><div>
        <p><b>q<sub>v</sub></b> = −(0.22 kJ/°C)(0.6 °C) = −0.132 kJ = <b>−132 J</b> (negative because the reaction released the heat the calorimeter absorbed).</p>
        <p><b>ΔE</b> per mole = −132 J ÷ 10.3 mg × 1000 mg/g × 122 g/mol = <b>−1563 kJ/mol</b>.</p>
        <p><b>ΔH</b>: C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>COOH(s) + 15/2 O<sub>2</sub> → 7CO<sub>2</sub>(g) + 3H<sub>2</sub>O(l); Δn<sub>g</sub> = 7 − 7.5 = −0.5. ΔH = −1563 + (−0.5)(8.314 × 10<sup>−3</sup>)(298) = <b>−1564 kJ/mol</b>.</p>
        <p><b>q<sub>p</sub></b> for 10.3 mg at constant pressure = −1564 kJ/mol × (10.3 × 10<sup>−3</sup> g/122 g/mol) = <b>−0.132 kJ</b>. Almost the same, because Δn<sub>g</sub> is small.</p>
      </div></details>
      <details class="worked"><summary>Handout Worked Example 2: 0.14 g CH<sub>3</sub>OH burned at constant pressure raises T by 0.222 °C; C<sub>p</sub>(cal) = 1.5 kJ/°C. Find q<sub>p</sub> and ΔH.</summary><div>
        <p>q<sub>p</sub> = −(1.5)(0.222) = <b>−0.333 kJ</b>. Moles = 0.14/32 = 4.38 × 10<sup>−3</sup> mol. ΔH = −0.333/4.38 × 10<sup>−3</sup> = <b>−76 kJ/mol</b>.</p>
        <p class="hint">Method check: the accepted enthalpy of combustion of methanol is about −726 kJ/mol, so the handout's sample data are best treated as a method exercise. Follow the same steps on your exam.</p>
      </div></details>
    </div>
    <div class="k">Why</div>
    <div class="v why">
      <p>The calorimeter and the reaction form an (approximately) isolated system together, so by the first law the heat the reaction releases is exactly the heat the calorimeter absorbs: q<sub>rxn</sub> = −q<sub>cal</sub>. The sign flip is where most errors happen. A temperature <i>rise</i> means the reaction is <i>exothermic</i>.</p>
      <p>The bomb measures ΔE because rigid walls make ΔV = 0, so no PV work is possible and all the energy change shows up as heat. The cup measures ΔH because the pressure is fixed by the atmosphere. Convert between them with Δn<sub>g</sub>RT.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="ph"><b>Health link</b>The Calories on Philippine food labels are kilocalories measured by burning food in bomb calorimeters (then corrected for digestion). 1 food Calorie = 1 kcal = 4.184 kJ. A 2000-kcal daily intake is about 8.4 MJ.</div>
</article>

