<article class="topic" id="d-form" aria-labelledby="t6">
  <header><span class="eyebrow">Lesson 6 · Evaluating ΔH, method 1</span><h2 id="t6">Heats of formation, ΔH°<sub>f</sub></h2></header>
  <div class="wh">
    <div class="k">What</div>
    <div class="v">
      <p>The <b>standard heat of formation</b> is the enthalpy change when <b>1 mole</b> of a compound forms from its elements in their standard states (25 °C, 1 bar). By definition, <b>ΔH°<sub>f</sub> of a pure element in its standard state is zero</b> (O<sub>2</sub>(g), H<sub>2</sub>(g), C(graphite), Na(s)…).</p>
      <div class="eq">ΔH°<sub>rxn</sub> = Σ n ΔH°<sub>f</sub>(products) − Σ m ΔH°<sub>f</sub>(reactants)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="k">How</div>
    <div class="v">
      <details class="worked" open><summary>Handout: enthalpy of combustion of C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub>(g). ΔH°<sub>f</sub>: C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub> +12.4, CO<sub>2</sub> −94.0, H<sub>2</sub>O(l) −68.3 kcal/mol</summary><div>
        <p>C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub>(g) + 3O<sub>2</sub>(g) → 2CO<sub>2</sub>(g) + 2H<sub>2</sub>O(l)</p>
        <p>ΔH = [2(−94.0) + 2(−68.3)] − [12.4 + 3(0)] = −324.6 − 12.4 = <b>−337 kcal</b>.</p>
      </div></details>
      <details class="worked"><summary>Past quiz: ΔH°<sub>f</sub> of glucose from ΔH<sub>comb</sub> = −2816 kJ/mol, ΔH°<sub>f</sub> CO<sub>2</sub> = −393.5, H<sub>2</sub>O(l) = −285.9 kJ/mol</summary><div>
        <p>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>6</sub>(s) + 6O<sub>2</sub>(g) → 6CO<sub>2</sub>(g) + 6H<sub>2</sub>O(l), ΔH = −2816 kJ.</p>
        <p>−2816 = [6(−393.5) + 6(−285.9)] − [ΔH°<sub>f</sub>(glucose) + 0] = −4076.4 − ΔH°<sub>f</sub>.</p>
        <p>ΔH°<sub>f</sub>(glucose) = −4076.4 + 2816 = <b>−1260 kJ/mol</b>. The unknown can sit on either side; just solve the same equation for it.</p>
      </div></details>
      <ul class="tight"><li>Multiply each ΔH°<sub>f</sub> by its coefficient.</li><li>Watch the phase: H<sub>2</sub>O(l) is −285.8 kJ/mol but H<sub>2</sub>O(g) is −241.8 kJ/mol.</li><li>Elements contribute zero, but do not forget to write them in.</li></ul>
    </div>
    <div class="k">Why</div>
    <div class="v why">
      <p>Because H is a state function, you can imagine any reaction as two steps: break all reactants down into elements (the reverse of their formation, so −ΣΔH°<sub>f</sub> reactants), then build the products from those same elements (+ΣΔH°<sub>f</sub> products). The elements are a common reference "sea level," which is exactly why their ΔH°<sub>f</sub> is set to zero. "Products minus reactants" is just Hess's law with formation reactions as the steps.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="seeit"><span>See it</span><a href="#v-fr">ΔH°f route to scale</a></p>
</article>

