<article class="topic" id="d-hess" aria-labelledby="t7">
  <header><span class="eyebrow">Lesson 7 · Evaluating ΔH, method 2</span><h2 id="t7">Hess's law</h2></header>
  <div class="wh">
    <div class="k">What</div>
    <div class="v">
      <p><b>Hess's law</b> (the law of heat summation): if a reaction can be written as the sum of several steps, its ΔH is the sum of the steps' ΔH values. ΔH does not depend on the route taken.</p>
      <ul class="tight">
        <li><b>Reverse</b> an equation → change the sign of its ΔH.</li>
        <li><b>Multiply</b> an equation by a factor → multiply its ΔH by the same factor.</li>
        <li><b>Add</b> the equations, cancel species that appear on both sides, and add the ΔH values.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="k">How</div>
    <div class="v">
      <p>Work from the <b>target</b> equation. For each target species, find the given equation that contains it, and flip or scale that equation so the species lands on the correct side with the correct coefficient. Species that appear in no target position must cancel out.</p>
      <details class="worked" open><summary>Handout: C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub>(g) + H<sub>2</sub>(g) → C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub>(g), from combustion data</summary><div>
        <p>Given: (1) C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub> + 7/2 O<sub>2</sub> → 2CO<sub>2</sub> + 3H<sub>2</sub>O, ΔH = −368.4 kcal; (2) C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub> + 3O<sub>2</sub> → 2CO<sub>2</sub> + 2H<sub>2</sub>O, −337.2 kcal; (3) H<sub>2</sub> + ½O<sub>2</sub> → H<sub>2</sub>O, −68.32 kcal.</p>
        <p>C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub> must be a <i>product</i>, so <b>reverse (1)</b>: +368.4 kcal. C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub> and H<sub>2</sub> are already reactants in (2) and (3), so keep them.</p>
        <p>Add: 2CO<sub>2</sub>, 3H<sub>2</sub>O and 7/2 O<sub>2</sub> all cancel. ΔH = +368.4 − 337.2 − 68.32 = <b>−37.1 kcal</b>.</p>
      </div></details>
      <p>Lab 3 in Remember lets you do this one (and a harder one) by clicking reverse and ×2 / ×½.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="k">Why</div>
    <div class="v why">
      <p>Hess's law is just the state-function property of Lesson 1 applied to reactions. Enthalpy is like altitude: going from reactants to products by one big step or by three small steps ends at the same height. Reversing a step means walking downhill instead of up, so the sign flips; doing a step twice doubles the climb. This is what lets chemists find ΔH for reactions that are too slow, too dangerous or impossible to run directly, like C(graphite) + 2H<sub>2</sub> → CH<sub>4</sub>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="seeit"><span>See it</span><a href="#v-fr">Two-step route</a><a href="#v-bh">Born–Haber staircase</a></p>
</article>

<!-- 8 -->
<article class="topic" id="d-bond" aria-labelledby="t8">
  <header><span class="eyebrow">Lesson 8 · Evaluating ΔH, method 3</span><h2 id="t8">Bond enthalpy and the Born–Haber cycle</h2></header>
  <div class="wh">
    <div class="k">What</div>
    <div class="v">
      <p><b>Bond enthalpy</b> is the energy required to break 1 mole of a given bond in the gas phase. It is always positive.</p>
      <ul class="tight"><li>Bond breaking: ΔH = <span class="sign p">+</span> (energy goes in).</li><li>Bond forming: ΔH = <span class="sign n">−</span> (energy comes out).</li></ul>
      <div class="eq">ΔH<sub>rxn</sub> ≈ Σ(bond enthalpies of bonds broken) − Σ(bond enthalpies of bonds formed)</div>
      <p>The <b>Born–Haber cycle</b> applies the same idea to forming an ionic solid from a metal and a nonmetal. It is used mainly to calculate <b>lattice energy (U)</b>, which cannot be measured directly.</p>
      <div class="eq">ΔH°<sub>f</sub> = ΔH<sub>sub</sub> + IE + ΔH<sub>diss</sub> + EA + U</div>
    </div>
    <div class="k">How</div>
    <div class="v">
      <details class="worked" open><summary>Handout: combustion of propane from bond enthalpies (C–H 413, C–C 347, O=O 498, C=O 805, O–H 464 kJ/mol)</summary><div>
        <p>CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub> + 5O<sub>2</sub> → 3CO<sub>2</sub> + 4H<sub>2</sub>O</p>
        <p><b>Broken:</b> 8 C–H (8 × 413) + 2 C–C (2 × 347) + 5 O=O (5 × 498) = +6488 kJ.</p>
        <p><b>Formed:</b> 6 C=O (two per CO<sub>2</sub>, 6 × 805) + 8 O–H (two per H<sub>2</sub>O, 8 × 464) = 8542 kJ released.</p>
        <p>ΔH = 6488 − 8542 = <b>−2054 kJ</b>. Compare with −2043 kJ from heats of formation: close but not identical.</p>
      </div></details>
      <details class="worked"><summary>Handout: Born–Haber cycle for NaCl</summary><div>
        <p>Na(s) → Na(g): ΔH<sub>sub</sub> = +108 kJ. ½Cl<sub>2</sub>(g) → Cl(g): ΔH<sub>diss</sub> = +122 kJ (half the Cl–Cl bond enthalpy). Na(g) → Na<sup>+</sup>(g) + e<sup>−</sup>: IE = +496 kJ. Cl(g) + e<sup>−</sup> → Cl<sup>−</sup>(g): EA = −349 kJ. Na<sup>+</sup>(g) + Cl<sup>−</sup>(g) → NaCl(s): U = −788 kJ.</p>
        <p>ΔH°<sub>f</sub> = 108 + 496 + 122 − 349 − 788 = <b>−411 kJ/mol</b>. On an exam, you are usually given ΔH°<sub>f</sub> and asked for U: rearrange the same sum.</p>
      </div></details>
    </div>
    <div class="k">Why</div>
    <div class="v why">
      <p>A reaction is exothermic when the bonds formed are stronger overall than the bonds broken. Breaking always costs energy and forming always pays, so the sign of ΔH tells you which side has the stronger bonds.</p>
      <p>Bond enthalpy answers are <b>approximate</b> because tabulated values are averages over many molecules (a C–H bond in methane is not identical to one in propane) and they assume everything is a gas. That is why −2054 kJ differs from the −2043 kJ formation-data value. When ΔH°<sub>f</sub> data are available, they give the more accurate answer.</p>
      <p>In the Born–Haber cycle, ionization is uphill and only partly repaid by electron affinity. The huge release of lattice energy, from oppositely charged ions packing together, is what makes forming NaCl favorable overall.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="seeit"><span>See it</span><a href="#v-bh">Born–Haber staircase</a></p>
</article>

