<article class="topic" id="d-phase" aria-labelledby="t9">
  <header><span class="eyebrow">Lesson 9</span><h2 id="t9">Heats of physical transformations</h2></header>
  <div class="wh">
    <div class="k">What</div>
    <div class="v">
      <p>Phase changes happen at constant temperature (they are <b>isothermal</b>), yet they absorb or release heat. Going solid → liquid → gas is endothermic; the reverse is exothermic.</p>
      <div class="eq block">ΔH<sub>sub</sub> = ΔH<sub>fus</sub> + ΔH<sub>vap</sub> &nbsp;&nbsp;(Hess's law)<br>ΔH<sub>freezing</sub> = −ΔH<sub>fus</sub> · ΔH<sub>condensation</sub> = −ΔH<sub>vap</sub> · ΔH<sub>deposition</sub> = −ΔH<sub>sub</sub><br>q = n·ΔH<sub>transition</sub> (during the plateau) &nbsp;·&nbsp; q = m·c·ΔT (while warming one phase)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="k">How</div>
    <div class="v">
      <p>On a <b>heating curve</b>, sloped segments warm a single phase (use mcΔT); flat segments are phase changes (use nΔH). Add the segments for the total heat. Lab 4 in Remember draws the water curve from −10 °C to 120 °C.</p>
      <details class="worked" open><summary>Handout Worked Example 3: vaporize 1.0 kg of water at 100 °C and 1 atm (ΔH<sub>vap</sub> = 40.7 kJ/mol). Find q, w, ΔH, ΔE.</summary><div>
        <p>n = 1000 g ÷ 18 g/mol = 55.6 mol.</p>
        <p><b>q<sub>p</sub></b> = nΔH<sub>vap</sub> = 55.6 × 40.7 = <b>2260 kJ</b>. ΔT = 0, but q is not zero: the heat separates molecules rather than raising T.</p>
        <p><b>w</b> = −P<sub>ext</sub>(V<sub>gas</sub> − V<sub>liq</sub>) ≈ −nRT (V<sub>liq</sub> is negligible) = −(55.6)(8.314)(373) = <b>−172 kJ</b>.</p>
        <p><b>ΔH</b> = q<sub>p</sub> = <b>2261 kJ</b>. <b>ΔE</b> = q + w = 2260 − 172 = <b>2088 kJ</b>.</p>
      </div></details>
      <details class="worked"><summary>Past quiz: enthalpy of sublimation of 100 g CO<sub>2</sub> at 183 K, ΔH<sub>sub</sub> = 3.87 kcal/mol</summary><div>
        <p>n = 100 g ÷ 44.01 g/mol = 2.27 mol. ΔH = 2.27 × 3.87 = <b>8.80 kcal</b>. Enthalpy is extensive: scale the molar value by the moles.</p>
      </div></details>
    </div>
    <div class="k">Why</div>
    <div class="v why">
      <p>During melting or boiling, every bit of added energy goes into overcoming intermolecular forces (increasing potential energy), not into making molecules move faster (kinetic energy, which is what temperature measures). So T stays flat until the whole sample has changed phase.</p>
      <p>ΔH<sub>vap</sub> is much larger than ΔH<sub>fus</sub> (40.7 vs 6.01 kJ/mol for water) because melting only loosens the hydrogen-bond network, while boiling must separate molecules completely. That is also why steam burns are worse than boiling-water burns: condensing steam on skin releases the large ΔH<sub>vap</sub> before the water even starts cooling.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="seeit"><span>See it</span><a href="#v-st">S vs T jumps</a><a href="#v-tr">Trouton's rule</a></p>
</article>

<!-- 10 -->
<article class="topic" id="d-spont" aria-labelledby="t10">
  <header><span class="eyebrow">Lesson 10</span><h2 id="t10">Spontaneity, entropy, and the second law</h2></header>
  <div class="wh">
    <div class="k">What</div>
    <div class="v">
      <p>A <b>spontaneous process</b> happens on its own, without continuous outside input of energy, once it is allowed to start. Examples from your handout:</p>
      <ul class="tight">
        <li>A gas expands into an evacuated flask, but never gathers back into one flask on its own.</li>
        <li>An iron nail rusts, but rust never turns back into shiny iron by itself.</li>
        <li>Ice melts above 0 °C, while water freezes below 0 °C. Spontaneity can depend on temperature.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><b>Entropy (S)</b> measures the dispersal of energy and matter, often described as disorder or randomness. More precisely, it counts the number of microscopic arrangements (microstates, W) consistent with a state: S = k ln W.</p>
      <p><b>Second law:</b> in any spontaneous process, the entropy of the universe increases.</p>
      <div class="eq">ΔS<sub>univ</sub> = ΔS<sub>sys</sub> + ΔS<sub>surr</sub> &gt; 0 (spontaneous) &nbsp;·&nbsp; = 0 (equilibrium) &nbsp;·&nbsp; &lt; 0 (nonspontaneous)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="k">How</div>
    <div class="v">
      <p>Entropy <b>increases</b> when:</p>
      <ul class="tight">
        <li>a substance melts, vaporizes or sublimes (S<sub>solid</sub> &lt; S<sub>liquid</sub> ≪ S<sub>gas</sub>);</li>
        <li>temperature rises (more thermal motion);</li>
        <li>the number of moles, especially of <b>gas</b>, increases: 2H<sub>2</sub>O(l) → 2H<sub>2</sub>(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g);</li>
        <li>a solid dissolves or ionizes: NaCl(s) → Na<sup>+</sup>(aq) + Cl<sup>−</sup>(aq);</li>
        <li>molecules get larger or more complex (more ways to vibrate and rotate).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>When gases are involved, Δn<sub>g</sub> is usually the deciding factor.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="k">Why</div>
    <div class="v why">
      <p>Spontaneous change is overwhelmingly a matter of probability. If gas molecules can occupy two flasks, there are astronomically more arrangements with them spread across both than with all of them in one. The system drifts toward the state with the most microstates simply because that state is far more likely. Nothing pushes the gas back, because that would mean wandering into a vanishingly rare arrangement.</p>
      <p>Why can exothermic processes be spontaneous even when the system's entropy drops (like water freezing)? Heat released into the surroundings increases <i>their</i> entropy: ΔS<sub>surr</sub> = −ΔH<sub>sys</sub>/T. Below 0 °C that gain outweighs the system's loss, so ΔS<sub>univ</sub> &gt; 0. The 1/T explains the temperature dependence: the same heat makes a bigger entropy difference to cold surroundings than to hot ones.</p>
      <p><b>Spontaneous does not mean fast.</b> Diamond turning into graphite is spontaneous at room temperature but takes geological time. Thermodynamics says whether a process can happen; kinetics says how fast.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="seeit"><span>See it</span><a href="#v-ms">Counting microstates</a><a href="#v-un">Entropy ledger</a></p>
</article>

<!-- 11 -->
<article class="topic" id="d-ent" aria-labelledby="t11">
  <header><span class="eyebrow">Lesson 11</span><h2 id="t11">Calculating entropy changes and the third law</h2></header>
  <div class="wh">
    <div class="k">What</div>
    <div class="v">
      <div class="eq block">Phase change (reversible, constant T): ΔS = q<sub>rev</sub>/T = ΔH<sub>transition</sub>/T<br>Reaction: ΔS°<sub>rxn</sub> = Σ n S°(products) − Σ m S°(reactants)</div>
      <p><b>Third law:</b> the entropy of a pure, perfect crystalline substance is zero at absolute zero (0 K). This gives every substance an <b>absolute</b> entropy S°, unlike E and H, which only have changes.</p>
      <p><b>Standard state</b> (IUPAC, 1982): 25 °C, 1 bar = 10<sup>5</sup> Pa = 0.9869 atm. Units of S°: J/mol·K. Unlike ΔH°<sub>f</sub>, the S° of an element is <b>not</b> zero at 25 °C.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="k">How</div>
    <div class="v">
      <details class="worked" open><summary>Handout: ΔS for vaporizing water at 100 °C, ΔH<sub>vap</sub> = 40.7 kJ/mol</summary><div>
        <p>ΔS = 4.07 × 10<sup>4</sup> J/mol ÷ 373 K = <b>+109.1 J/mol·K</b>. Use kelvin and convert kJ to J.</p>
      </div></details>
      <details class="worked"><summary>Handout: ΔS° for O<sub>2</sub>(g) + 2H<sub>2</sub>(g) → 2H<sub>2</sub>O(g). S°: O<sub>2</sub> 205.0, H<sub>2</sub> 130.6, H<sub>2</sub>O(g) 188.7 J/mol·K</summary><div>
        <p>ΔS° = 2(188.7) − [205.0 + 2(130.6)] = 377.4 − 466.2 = <b>−88.8 J/K</b>. Negative: 3 mol of gas become 2.</p>
      </div></details>
      <details class="worked"><summary>Handout: ΔS° for CH<sub>4</sub>(g) + 2O<sub>2</sub>(g) → CO<sub>2</sub>(g) + 2H<sub>2</sub>O(l). S°: CH<sub>4</sub> 186.2, O<sub>2</sub> 205.0, CO<sub>2</sub> 213.6, H<sub>2</sub>O(l) 69.9</summary><div>
        <p>ΔS° = [213.6 + 2(69.9)] − [186.2 + 2(205.0)] = 353.4 − 596.2 = <b>−242.8 J/K</b>. Strongly negative: 3 mol of gas become 1 mol of gas plus liquid.</p>
      </div></details>
    </div>
    <div class="k">Why</div>
    <div class="v why">
      <p><b>What zero entropy means</b> (the handout's question): a perfect crystal at 0 K has every particle fixed in one position with no thermal motion. There is exactly one possible arrangement, W = 1, so S = k ln 1 = 0. Any warmth or imperfection adds arrangements and raises S. The handout's S-versus-T graph shows S rising steadily within each phase and jumping at melting and boiling, where positional freedom suddenly increases. The jump at boiling is far larger.</p>
      <p>Why ΔS = q<sub>rev</sub>/T: adding heat to a cold system creates relatively more new microstates than adding the same heat to a hot, already disordered system. Dividing by T captures that.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="seeit"><span>See it</span><a href="#v-ms">Microstates</a><a href="#v-st">Entropy of water</a><a href="#v-tr">Trouton analysis</a></p>
</article>

