<article class="topic" id="d-gibbs" aria-labelledby="t12">
  <header><span class="eyebrow">Lesson 12</span><h2 id="t12">Gibbs free energy</h2></header>
  <div class="wh">
    <div class="k">What</div>
    <div class="v">
      <p><b>Gibbs free energy</b> is the maximum energy available in a system to do useful work. At constant temperature and pressure:</p>
      <div class="eq">ΔG = ΔH − TΔS &nbsp;&nbsp; (T in kelvin; put ΔH and TΔS in the same units)</div>
      <ul class="tight">
        <li>ΔG &lt; 0: <b>spontaneous</b> as written.</li>
        <li>ΔG &gt; 0: <b>nonspontaneous</b>; needs a continuous input of energy (the reverse is spontaneous).</li>
        <li>ΔG = 0: at <b>equilibrium</b>; no net reactants or products are formed.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="tbl"><table>
        <caption>The four sign cases</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">ΔH</th><th scope="col">ΔS</th><th scope="col">ΔG = ΔH − TΔS</th><th scope="col">Spontaneous…</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><span class="sign n">−</span></td><td><span class="sign p">+</span></td><td>always −</td><td>at all temperatures</td><td>Combustion of many fuels; 2H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> → 2H<sub>2</sub>O + O<sub>2</sub></td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="sign p">+</span></td><td><span class="sign n">−</span></td><td>always +</td><td>never</td><td>3O<sub>2</sub> → 2O<sub>3</sub></td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="sign n">−</span></td><td><span class="sign n">−</span></td><td>− at low T</td><td>below T = ΔH/ΔS</td><td>Water freezing; N<sub>2</sub> + 3H<sub>2</sub> → 2NH<sub>3</sub></td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="sign p">+</span></td><td><span class="sign p">+</span></td><td>− at high T</td><td>above T = ΔH/ΔS</td><td>Ice melting; CaCO<sub>3</sub> → CaO + CO<sub>2</sub></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
    </div>
    <div class="k">How</div>
    <div class="v">
      <details class="worked" open><summary>Handout: ΔG for 2H<sub>2</sub>O(l) → 2H<sub>2</sub>(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) at 25 °C, ΔH° = 571.6 kJ, ΔS° = 326.4 J/K</summary><div>
        <p>T = 25 + 273.15 = 298 K. ΔS = 0.3264 kJ/K.</p>
        <p>ΔG = 571.6 − (298)(0.3264) = 571.6 − 97.3 = <b>+474.3 kJ</b>. Nonspontaneous: you need electrolysis to split water.</p>
      </div></details>
      <details class="worked"><summary>Past quiz: 2A(g) + B(g) → 2D(g), ΔE°<sub>298</sub> = −2.50 kcal, ΔS°<sub>298</sub> = −10.5 cal/K. ΔG°? (R = 1.987 cal/mol·K)</summary><div>
        <p><b>Step 1, ΔE → ΔH:</b> Δn<sub>g</sub> = 2 − 3 = −1. ΔH = ΔE + Δn<sub>g</sub>RT = −2500 cal + (−1)(1.987)(298) = −2500 − 592 = −3092 cal.</p>
        <p><b>Step 2, ΔG:</b> ΔG = −3092 − (298)(−10.5) = −3092 + 3129 = <b>+36.9 cal</b>, so it is <b>nonspontaneous</b>.</p>
        <p>The trap is using ΔE directly: −2500 + 3129 = +629 cal. You must convert to ΔH first, because ΔG is defined with H.</p>
      </div></details>
      <p><b>Crossover temperature:</b> for the same-sign cases, set ΔG = 0: T = ΔH/ΔS. For CaCO<sub>3</sub> → CaO + CO<sub>2</sub> (ΔH° = +178.1 kJ, ΔS° = +160.5 J/K), T = 178 100/160.5 ≈ 1110 K. A lime kiln must run hotter than about 840 °C.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="k">Why</div>
    <div class="v why">
      <p>ΔG is the second law expressed in terms of the system alone. Start from ΔS<sub>univ</sub> = ΔS<sub>sys</sub> + ΔS<sub>surr</sub>, with ΔS<sub>surr</sub> = −ΔH/T. Multiply by −T and you get −TΔS<sub>univ</sub> = ΔH − TΔS = ΔG. So ΔG &lt; 0 is exactly the same statement as ΔS<sub>univ</sub> &gt; 0, but you only need data about the system.</p>
      <p>The equation is a tug-of-war: ΔH (the drive toward lower energy) against TΔS (the drive toward more disorder). Temperature is the referee's weight on the entropy side, which is why the entropy term wins at high T. ΔG says nothing about rate: a free-energy diagram can show products far downhill behind a high activation hill.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="ext"><b>Looking ahead: ΔG and equilibrium</b>Not in this handout, but it links to your equilibrium unit: ΔG = ΔG° + RT ln Q, and at equilibrium (ΔG = 0, Q = K) this gives ΔG° = −RT ln K. A negative ΔG° means K &gt; 1 (products favored). This is how thermodynamic tables predict equilibrium constants.</div>
  <div class="ph"><b>Biology link</b>Cells run nonspontaneous reactions by coupling them to spontaneous ones. Attaching phosphate to glucose has ΔG°′ ≈ +13.8 kJ/mol; ATP hydrolysis has ΔG°′ ≈ −30.5 kJ/mol. Coupled, the net ΔG°′ is about −16.7 kJ/mol, so glucose can be trapped in the cell to start glycolysis. Because G is a state function, the ΔG values simply add.</div>
  <p class="seeit"><span>See it</span><a href="#v-un">ΔG = −TΔS(univ)</a><a href="#v-q4">ΔH–ΔS map</a><a href="#v-gx">G vs extent → K</a></p>
</article>

<div class="part" aria-hidden="true">Chemical equilibrium</div>
<!-- A1 -->
<article class="topic" id="d-dyn" aria-labelledby="ta1">
  <header><span class="eyebrow">Lesson 13 · Chemical equilibrium</span><h2 id="ta1">Reversible reactions and dynamic equilibrium</h2></header>
  <div class="wh">
    <div class="k">What</div>
    <div class="v">
      <p>Many reactions go to completion, stopping when one reactant runs out. Others do not, because the products can react to re-form the reactants. A <b>reversible reaction</b> runs in both the forward (left → right) and reverse (right → left) directions, shown by a double arrow ⇌.</p>
      <div class="eq">H<sub>2</sub>(g) + I<sub>2</sub>(g) ⇌ 2HI(g) &nbsp;&nbsp;·&nbsp;&nbsp; N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub>(g) ⇌ 2NO<sub>2</sub>(g)</div>
      <p><b>Chemical equilibrium</b> is reached when the rate of the forward reaction equals the rate of the reverse reaction. Concentrations then stop changing, but both reactions keep going. That is why it is called a <b>dynamic</b> equilibrium.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="k">How</div>
    <div class="v">
      <p>Your handout's characteristics of a system at equilibrium:</p>
      <ul class="tight">
        <li>It is a <b>dynamic</b> process: forward and reverse reactions continue indefinitely.</li>
        <li>It can only be reached in a <b>closed system</b> (nothing added or removed). In an open flask, escaping gas would keep pulling the reaction one way.</li>
        <li>The final equilibrium position is the <b>same whether you start from reactants or products</b>. Pure N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub> or pure NO<sub>2</sub>, the system ends in the same mixture of both (at the same T and total composition).</li>
        <li>The macroscopic properties (color, pressure, concentrations) stay constant.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>In the handout's graph, [N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub>] falls and [NO<sub>2</sub>] rises until they level off at 0.75 M and 0.50 M. The flat lines mark equilibrium.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="k">Why</div>
    <div class="v why">
      <p>At the start, there is lots of N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub> and no NO<sub>2</sub>, so the forward rate (k<sub>f</sub>[N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub>]) is high and the reverse rate (k<sub>r</sub>[NO<sub>2</sub>]<sup>2</sup>) is zero. As N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub> is used up, the forward rate drops; as NO<sub>2</sub> builds up, the reverse rate rises. They must meet. When they do, every N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub> that splits is matched by two NO<sub>2</sub> that recombine, so nothing changes overall.</p>
      <p>Setting rate<sub>f</sub> = rate<sub>r</sub> gives k<sub>f</sub>[N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub>] = k<sub>r</sub>[NO<sub>2</sub>]<sup>2</sup>, so k<sub>f</sub>/k<sub>r</sub> = [NO<sub>2</sub>]<sup>2</sup>/[N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub>] = K. The equilibrium constant is a ratio of two rate constants, which is why it depends only on temperature.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="note"><b>Common misconception</b>Equal <i>rates</i> do not mean equal <i>concentrations</i>. In most equilibria the concentrations are quite different (0.75 M vs 0.50 M in the example).</div>
  <p class="seeit"><span>See it</span><a href="#v-ap">Reaching equilibrium</a></p>
</article>

<!-- A2 -->
<article class="topic" id="d-mass" aria-labelledby="ta2">
  <header><span class="eyebrow">Lesson 14 · Chemical equilibrium</span><h2 id="ta2">The law of mass action: K<sub>c</sub>, K<sub>p</sub>, and Q</h2></header>
  <div class="wh">
    <div class="k">What</div>
    <div class="v">
      <p>For aA + bB ⇌ cC + dD, at equilibrium:</p>
      <div class="eq block">K<sub>c</sub> = [C]<sup>c</sup>[D]<sup>d</sup> / [A]<sup>a</sup>[B]<sup>b</sup> &nbsp; (molar concentrations; solutions or gases)<br>K<sub>p</sub> = (P<sub>C</sub>)<sup>c</sup>(P<sub>D</sub>)<sup>d</sup> / (P<sub>A</sub>)<sup>a</sup>(P<sub>B</sub>)<sup>b</sup> &nbsp; (partial pressures in atm; gases)<br><b>K<sub>p</sub> = K<sub>c</sub>(RT)<sup>Δn</sup></b> &nbsp; R = 0.0821 L·atm/mol·K, T in K, Δn = (c + d) − (a + b) for <b>gases only</b></div>
      <ul class="tight">
        <li><b>Homogeneous equilibrium:</b> everything is in the same phase (all gases, or all aqueous).</li>
        <li><b>Heterogeneous equilibrium:</b> more than one phase. Pure solids and pure liquids are <b>left out</b> of K: PbS(s) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l) ⇌ Pb<sup>2+</sup> + S<sup>2−</sup>, K<sub>c</sub> = [Pb<sup>2+</sup>][S<sup>2−</sup>]; FeO(s) + CO(g) ⇌ Fe(s) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g), K<sub>p</sub> = P<sub>CO<sub>2</sub></sub>/P<sub>CO</sub>.</li>
        <li><b>Size of K:</b> K ≫ 1 favors products; K ≪ 1 favors reactants.</li>
        <li><b>K<sub>c</sub> = K<sub>p</sub></b> only when Δn = 0, because (RT)<sup>0</sup> = 1.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="tbl"><table>
        <caption>Manipulating K</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Change to the equation</th><th scope="col">New K</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Reverse it</th><td>1/K</td><td>If A ⇌ B has K = 4, then B ⇌ A has K = 0.25</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Multiply coefficients by n</th><td>K<sup>n</sup></td><td>2SO<sub>2</sub> + O<sub>2</sub> ⇌ 2SO<sub>3</sub>: K<sub>p</sub> = 1.6 × 10<sup>4</sup>; halved equation: √(1.6 × 10<sup>4</sup>) = 1.3 × 10<sup>2</sup></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Add two equations</th><td>K<sub>1</sub> × K<sub>2</sub></td><td>AgBr(s) + 2NH<sub>3</sub> ⇌ Ag(NH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup> + Br<sup>−</sup>: K = K<sub>sp</sub> × K<sub>f</sub></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p><b>Reaction quotient, Q:</b> the same expression as K, but using the concentrations at <i>any</i> moment. Q &lt; K → the reaction proceeds forward; Q &gt; K → it proceeds in reverse; Q = K → at equilibrium.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="k">How</div>
    <div class="v">
      <details class="worked" open><summary>Handout Question 1b: Haber process, K<sub>c</sub> = 3.5 × 10<sup>8</sup> at 25 °C. Find K<sub>p</sub>.</summary><div>
        <p>N<sub>2</sub>(g) + 3H<sub>2</sub>(g) ⇌ 2NH<sub>3</sub>(g): Δn = 2 − (1 + 3) = −2. T = 298 K.</p>
        <p>K<sub>p</sub> = (3.5 × 10<sup>8</sup>)(0.0821 × 298)<sup>−2</sup> = 3.5 × 10<sup>8</sup> ÷ 598.6 = <b>5.8 × 10<sup>5</sup></b>.</p>
      </div></details>
      <details class="worked"><summary>Handout Question 2: 2A(aq) + 3B(aq) ⇌ C(aq) + 2D(aq) with [A] = 1, [B] = 2, [C] = 3, [D] = 4 M. Find K<sub>c</sub>.</summary><div>
        <p>K<sub>c</sub> = [C][D]<sup>2</sup>/[A]<sup>2</sup>[B]<sup>3</sup> = (3)(4)<sup>2</sup>/(1)<sup>2</sup>(2)<sup>3</sup> = 48/8 = <b>6</b>.</p>
      </div></details>
      <details class="worked"><summary>Handout Question 3: rocket, 2H<sub>2</sub>(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) ⇌ 2H<sub>2</sub>O(g) at 3000 K; P(H<sub>2</sub>O) = 1.0, P(H<sub>2</sub>) = 1.5 × 10<sup>−3</sup>, P(O<sub>2</sub>) = 7.6 × 10<sup>−4</sup> atm. Find K<sub>c</sub>.</summary><div>
        <p>K<sub>p</sub> = (1.0)<sup>2</sup>/[(1.5 × 10<sup>−3</sup>)<sup>2</sup>(7.6 × 10<sup>−4</sup>)] = 5.8 × 10<sup>8</sup>.</p>
        <p>Δn = 2 − 3 = −1, so K<sub>p</sub> = K<sub>c</sub>(RT)<sup>−1</sup> and K<sub>c</sub> = K<sub>p</sub>(RT) = (5.8 × 10<sup>8</sup>)(0.0821)(3000) = <b>1.4 × 10<sup>11</sup></b>.</p>
      </div></details>
      <details class="worked"><summary>Handout: K<sub>c</sub> = 0.04 for PCl<sub>5</sub> ⇌ PCl<sub>3</sub> + Cl<sub>2</sub> at 250 °C. 0.20 mol PCl<sub>3</sub> and 0.12 mol Cl<sub>2</sub> in 4.000 L at equilibrium. Mass of PCl<sub>5</sub>?</summary><div>
        <p>[PCl<sub>3</sub>] = 0.20/4 = 0.05 M; [Cl<sub>2</sub>] = 0.12/4 = 0.03 M. [PCl<sub>5</sub>] = (0.05)(0.03)/0.04 = 0.0375 M.</p>
        <p>0.0375 mol/L × 208.5 g/mol × 4 L = <b>31.3 g</b>. Convert moles to <b>molarity</b> before using K<sub>c</sub>.</p>
      </div></details>
      <details class="worked"><summary>ICE table to find equilibrium concentrations: H<sub>2</sub> + I<sub>2</sub> ⇌ 2HI, K<sub>c</sub> = 50.5 at 448 °C, starting with 1.00 M H<sub>2</sub> and 1.00 M I<sub>2</sub></summary><div>
        <p>Change: −x, −x, +2x. 50.5 = (2x)<sup>2</sup>/(1.00 − x)<sup>2</sup>. Take the square root: 7.11 = 2x/(1.00 − x), so x = 0.780.</p>
        <p>[HI] = 1.56 M; [H<sub>2</sub>] = [I<sub>2</sub>] = 0.22 M. Check: (1.56)<sup>2</sup>/(0.22)<sup>2</sup> ≈ 50. ✓</p>
      </div></details>
    </div>
    <div class="k">Why</div>
    <div class="v why">
      <p>Pure solids and liquids are left out because their "concentration" (density divided by molar mass) is fixed: adding more solid does not make it more concentrated. Their constant values are absorbed into K.</p>
      <p>K<sub>c</sub> and K<sub>p</sub> differ because P = (n/V)RT = [X]RT for an ideal gas. Substituting [X]RT for every pressure leaves an extra factor of RT for each mole of gas, so the leftover is (RT)<sup>Δn</sup>.</p>
      <p>Why constants multiply when reactions add (your past quiz question): ΔG° values of added reactions add, and ΔG° = −RT ln K. So −RT ln K<sub>total</sub> = −RT(ln K<sub>1</sub> + ln K<sub>2</sub>) = −RT ln(K<sub>1</sub>K<sub>2</sub>), giving K<sub>total</sub> = K<sub>1</sub> × K<sub>2</sub>. Reversing negates ΔG°, which turns ln K into −ln K, i.e. 1/K.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="seeit"><span>See it</span><a href="#v-qk">Q vs K</a></p>
</article>

<!-- A3 -->
<article class="topic" id="d-gk" aria-labelledby="ta3">
  <header><span class="eyebrow">Lesson 15 · Chemical equilibrium</span><h2 id="ta3">Free energy and the equilibrium constant</h2></header>
  <div class="wh">
    <div class="k">What</div>
    <div class="v">
      <div class="eq block">ΔG = ΔG° + RT ln Q &nbsp;&nbsp; at equilibrium ΔG = 0 and Q = K, so <b>ΔG° = −RT ln K</b><br>R = 8.314 J/mol·K (ΔG° in J/mol) · K = e<sup>−ΔG°/RT</sup></div>
      <div class="tbl"><table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">K</th><th scope="col">At equilibrium</th><th scope="col">ΔG°</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>K &lt; 1</td><td>Reactants favored</td><td>&gt; 0 (+)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>K = 1</td><td>Neither favored</td><td>0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>K &gt; 1</td><td>Products favored</td><td>&lt; 0 (−)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
    </div>
    <div class="k">How</div>
    <div class="v">
      <details class="worked" open><summary>Handout Question 1a–b: N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub>(g) ⇌ 2NO<sub>2</sub>(g) at 298 K, ΔG°<sub>f</sub>: NO<sub>2</sub> 51.3, N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub> 99.8 kJ/mol</summary><div>
        <p>ΔG° = 2(51.3) − 99.8 = <b>+2.8 kJ/mol</b>.</p>
        <p>ln K = −ΔG°/RT = −2800/(8.314 × 298) = −1.130, so K = e<sup>−1.130</sup> = <b>0.323</b>. Convert kJ to J so the units cancel with R.</p>
      </div></details>
      <details class="worked"><summary>Handout: K = 0.51 for PCl<sub>5</sub> decomposition at 500 K. Find ΔG°.</summary><div>
        <p>ΔG° = −(8.314)(500) ln(0.51) = −(4157)(−0.673) = +2800 J/mol = <b>+2.8 kJ/mol</b>. Positive, consistent with K &lt; 1.</p>
      </div></details>
    </div>
    <div class="k">Why</div>
    <div class="v why">
      <p>ΔG° compares pure reactants with pure products in their standard states; K describes the mixture where the system actually settles. A reaction with a small positive ΔG° (like +2.8 kJ) still makes a noticeable amount of product (K = 0.32), because mixing reactants and products lowers G. Only a large positive ΔG° means "essentially no reaction." Every 5.7 kJ/mol of ΔG° at 25 °C changes K by a factor of 10.</p>
      <p class="hint">For gas reactions, the K obtained from ΔG° is strictly K<sub>p</sub> (standard state 1 bar). Your handout labels it K<sub>c</sub>; follow your instructor's labeling on exams.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="seeit"><span>See it</span><a href="#v-ap">Same K from both sides</a></p>
</article>

<!-- A4 -->
<article class="topic" id="d-lech" aria-labelledby="ta4">
  <header><span class="eyebrow">Lesson 16 · Chemical equilibrium</span><h2 id="ta4">Le Châtelier's principle</h2></header>
  <div class="wh">
    <div class="k">What</div>
    <div class="v">
      <p><b>Le Châtelier's principle</b> (1884): if a system at equilibrium is subjected to a stress, the position of the equilibrium shifts in the direction that relieves the stress.</p>
      <div class="tbl"><table>
        <caption>Stresses and responses</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Stress</th><th scope="col">Shift</th><th scope="col">Does K change?</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Add a reactant / remove a product</th><td>Forward (→)</td><td>No</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Add a product / remove a reactant</th><td>Reverse (←)</td><td>No</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Decrease volume (increase pressure)</th><td>Toward fewer moles of gas</td><td>No</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Increase volume (decrease pressure)</th><td>Toward more moles of gas</td><td>No</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Add inert gas at constant volume</th><td>No shift (partial pressures unchanged)</td><td>No</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Add inert gas at constant pressure</th><td>Toward more moles of gas (volume grows)</td><td>No</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Raise temperature</th><td>Endothermic direction</td><td><b>Yes</b>: K falls for exothermic, rises for endothermic reactions</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Add a catalyst</th><td>No shift; equilibrium is reached faster</td><td>No</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
    </div>
    <div class="k">How</div>
    <div class="v">
      <p>Treat heat as a reactant (endothermic) or a product (exothermic), then apply the same "add → shift away" logic.</p>
      <details class="worked" open><summary>Handout lab: the cobalt chloride equilibrium</summary><div>
        <div class="eq">[Co(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>2+</sup> (pink) + 4Cl<sup>−</sup> + heat ⇌ [CoCl<sub>4</sub>]<sup>2−</sup> (blue) + 6H<sub>2</sub>O</div>
        <ul class="tight">
          <li>Add HCl (more Cl<sup>−</sup>) → shifts right → <b>blue</b>.</li>
          <li>Add water → shifts left → <b>pink</b>.</li>
          <li>Heat (hot water bath) → shifts right → <b>blue</b>; ice bath → <b>pink</b>. So the forward reaction is endothermic: <b>Reaction A</b> in your handout (heat on the reactant side) matches the observed colors.</li>
          <li>Add AgNO<sub>3</sub> → AgCl precipitates, removing Cl<sup>−</sup> → shifts left → pink.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><b>Why HCl, and can NaCl or KCl be used?</b> All three supply the common ion Cl<sup>−</sup>, so in principle they push the equilibrium the same way. Concentrated HCl (about 12 M) delivers a very high [Cl<sup>−</sup>] in a few drops, giving a dramatic color change. Solid NaCl or KCl is limited by solubility (about 6 M at most) and dissolves slowly, so the shift is smaller and slower. H<sup>+</sup> itself plays no part in this equilibrium.</p>
        <p><b>Practical application:</b> a <b>humidity (moisture) indicator</b>, such as cobalt chloride paper or the blue silica-gel beads in packaging, which turn pink when they absorb water.</p>
      </div></details>
      <details class="worked"><summary>Handout lab: chromate–dichromate</summary><div>
        <div class="eq">2CrO<sub>4</sub><sup>2−</sup> (yellow) + 2H<sup>+</sup> ⇌ Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub><sup>2−</sup> (orange) + H<sub>2</sub>O</div>
        <p>Add acid (H<sup>+</sup>) → shifts right → orange. Add base: OH<sup>−</sup> removes H<sup>+</sup> → shifts left → yellow. It is the common-ion idea with H<sup>+</sup>.</p>
      </div></details>
      <details class="worked"><summary>Haber process: N<sub>2</sub> + 3H<sub>2</sub> ⇌ 2NH<sub>3</sub>, ΔH = −92 kJ</summary><div>
        <p>High pressure favors NH<sub>3</sub> (4 mol gas → 2). Low temperature favors NH<sub>3</sub> (exothermic), but makes the reaction too slow, so industry compromises at about 400–450 °C with an iron catalyst and removes NH<sub>3</sub> as it forms.</p>
      </div></details>
    </div>
    <div class="k">Why</div>
    <div class="v why">
      <p>Le Châtelier is a shortcut for comparing Q with K. Adding a reactant lowers Q below K, so the reaction runs forward until Q = K again. Squeezing a gas mixture changes every partial pressure by the same factor, but because the side with more gas moles has more pressure terms, Q changes and the system shifts toward fewer moles. Adding an inert gas at constant volume changes no partial pressure, so Q is untouched and nothing shifts.</p>
      <p>Temperature is different: it is the only stress that changes K itself, because K = k<sub>f</sub>/k<sub>r</sub> and the endothermic direction's rate constant grows faster with temperature (larger activation energy). A catalyst lowers both activation energies equally, so it speeds both directions without moving the equilibrium.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="seeit"><span>See it</span><a href="#v-lc">Concentration–time stress graph</a></p>
</article>


    </div>
  </div>
</section>

