<section class="view flow-lg" id="view-compare" aria-labelledby="h-cmp" hidden>
  <div class="flow">
    <span class="eyebrow">Comparative analysis</span>
    <h1 id="h-cmp">Side by side</h1>
    <p class="lede">Most lost points in thermodynamics come from a wrong sign or a mixed-up pair. Each table lines up two or more look-alikes row by row.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="flow" aria-labelledby="c1"><h2 id="c1">State functions vs path functions</h2>
  <div class="tbl"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">State function</th><th scope="col">Path function</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Depends on</th><td>Only the initial and final states</td><td>The route taken between them</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Examples</th><td>P, V, T, E, H, S, G</td><td>q, w</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Letter clue</th><td>Capital letters</td><td>Lowercase letters</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Mountain analogy</th><td>Change in altitude (4004 m either way)</td><td>Distance traveled (5662 m vs 9857 m)</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Consequence</th><td colspan="2">Because ΔH is a state function, Hess's law and Born–Haber cycles work. You cannot add up q values along different routes the same way.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div></section>

  <section class="flow" aria-labelledby="c2"><h2 id="c2">Heat vs work vs internal energy</h2>
  <div class="tbl"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">q (heat)</th><th scope="col">w (work)</th><th scope="col">ΔE (internal energy)</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">What it is</th><td>Energy transferred because of a temperature difference</td><td>Energy transferred when something is moved by a force (e.g. PΔV)</td><td>Change in total kinetic + potential energy of the particles</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Positive when</th><td>Heat absorbed by the system</td><td>Work done <b>on</b> the system (compression)</td><td>System gains energy</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Negative when</th><td>Heat released by the system</td><td>Work done <b>by</b> the system (expansion)</td><td>System loses energy</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Type</th><td>Path function</td><td>Path function</td><td>State function</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Zero when</th><td>Adiabatic process</td><td>Constant volume (no PΔV work)</td><td>Isothermal ideal gas; cyclic process</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div></section>

  <section class="flow" aria-labelledby="c3"><h2 id="c3">The four types of process (ideal gas)</h2>
  <div class="tbl"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Process</th><th scope="col">Constant</th><th scope="col">q</th><th scope="col">w</th><th scope="col">ΔE</th><th scope="col">ΔH</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Isobaric</th><td>P</td><td>nC<sub>p</sub>ΔT (= ΔH)</td><td>−PΔV = −nRΔT</td><td>nC<sub>v</sub>ΔT</td><td>q<sub>p</sub></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Isochoric</th><td>V</td><td>nC<sub>v</sub>ΔT (= ΔE)</td><td>0</td><td>q<sub>v</sub></td><td>ΔE + VΔP</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Isothermal (reversible)</th><td>T</td><td>−w</td><td>−nRT ln(V<sub>2</sub>/V<sub>1</sub>)</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Adiabatic</th><td>q = 0</td><td>0</td><td>= ΔE</td><td>w</td><td>ΔE + Δ(PV)</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div></section>

  <section class="flow" aria-labelledby="c4"><h2 id="c4">ΔE vs ΔH</h2>
  <div class="tbl"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">ΔE (or ΔU)</th><th scope="col">ΔH</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Equals the heat at</th><td>Constant volume: q<sub>v</sub></td><td>Constant pressure: q<sub>p</sub></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Measured by</th><td>Bomb calorimeter</td><td>Coffee-cup calorimeter</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Link</th><td colspan="2">ΔH = ΔE + Δn<sub>g</sub>RT (Δn<sub>g</sub> = gas moles of products − reactants)</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Δn<sub>g</sub> = 0</th><td colspan="2">ΔH = ΔE (e.g. H<sub>2</sub> + Cl<sub>2</sub> → 2HCl)</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Δn<sub>g</sub> &lt; 0</th><td colspan="2">ΔH more negative than ΔE (B<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub> combustion: −2143.2 → −2153.1 kJ)</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Δn<sub>g</sub> &gt; 0</th><td colspan="2">ΔH more positive than ΔE (some energy leaves as expansion work)</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div></section>

  <section class="flow" aria-labelledby="c5"><h2 id="c5">Bomb vs coffee-cup calorimeter</h2>
  <div class="tbl"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Bomb calorimeter</th><th scope="col">Coffee-cup calorimeter</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Condition</th><td>Constant volume (sealed steel)</td><td>Constant pressure (open to air)</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Work</th><td>w = 0</td><td>PΔV work possible</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Measures</th><td>q<sub>v</sub> = ΔE</td><td>q<sub>p</sub> = ΔH</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Typical use</th><td>Combustion, food Calories</td><td>Neutralization, dissolving, reactions in solution</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Heat equation</th><td>q<sub>rxn</sub> = −C<sub>cal</sub>ΔT</td><td>q<sub>rxn</sub> = −(m·c·ΔT) or −C<sub>cal</sub>ΔT</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div></section>

  <section class="flow" aria-labelledby="c6"><h2 id="c6">Reversible vs irreversible work</h2>
  <div class="tbl"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Irreversible</th><th scope="col">Reversible</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">External pressure</th><td>Constant P<sub>ext</sub>, lower than P<sub>gas</sub></td><td>P<sub>ext</sub> = P<sub>gas</sub> at every instant</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Formula</th><td>w = −P<sub>ext</sub>(V<sub>2</sub> − V<sub>1</sub>)</td><td>w = −nRT ln(V<sub>2</sub>/V<sub>1</sub>) (isothermal)</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Handout example (10 → 14 L)</th><td class="num">−405.3 J</td><td class="num">−682 J</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Magnitude</th><td>Smaller</td><td>Maximum possible work</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Real life</th><td>All real processes</td><td>An idealized limit (infinitely slow)</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div></section>

  <section class="flow" aria-labelledby="c7"><h2 id="c7">Four ways to get ΔH</h2>
  <div class="tbl"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Method</th><th scope="col">Formula</th><th scope="col">Needs</th><th scope="col">Accuracy</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Calorimetry</th><td>q = CΔT, then per mole</td><td>An experiment</td><td>Measured directly</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Heats of formation</th><td>ΣnΔH°<sub>f</sub>(prod) − ΣmΔH°<sub>f</sub>(react)</td><td>A ΔH°<sub>f</sub> table</td><td>Exact (within data)</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Hess's law</th><td>Add steps; reverse → flip sign; scale → scale ΔH</td><td>Known ΔH of related reactions</td><td>Exact</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Bond enthalpies</th><td>Σ broken − Σ formed</td><td>Average bond enthalpies, Lewis structures</td><td>Approximate (averages, gas phase)</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Born–Haber cycle</th><td>ΔH°<sub>f</sub> = ΔH<sub>sub</sub> + IE + ΔH<sub>diss</sub> + EA + U</td><td>Atomic data</td><td>Used to find lattice energy U</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div></section>

  <section class="flow" aria-labelledby="c8"><h2 id="c8">Elements in the data tables</h2>
  <div class="tbl"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Quantity for an element in its standard state</th><th scope="col">Value</th><th scope="col">Why</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">ΔH°<sub>f</sub></th><td>0</td><td>Defined as the reference point; forming an element from itself is no change</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">ΔG°<sub>f</sub></th><td>0</td><td>Same reference choice</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">S°</th><td><b>Not</b> zero (e.g. O<sub>2</sub> 205.0, H<sub>2</sub> 130.6 J/mol·K)</td><td>Entropy is absolute: only a perfect crystal at 0 K has S = 0 (third law)</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div></section>

  <section class="flow" aria-labelledby="c9"><h2 id="c9">The three laws</h2>
  <div class="tbl"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Law</th><th scope="col">Statement</th><th scope="col">Equation</th><th scope="col">Answers</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">First</th><td>Energy is neither created nor destroyed</td><td>ΔE = q + w; ΔE<sub>sys</sub> = −ΔE<sub>surr</sub></td><td>How much energy changes hands</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Second</th><td>The entropy of the universe increases in a spontaneous process</td><td>ΔS<sub>univ</sub> &gt; 0; ΔG &lt; 0</td><td>Which direction a process goes</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Third</th><td>A perfect crystal at 0 K has zero entropy</td><td>S(0 K) = 0</td><td>Where the entropy scale starts</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div></section>

  <section class="flow" aria-labelledby="c10"><h2 id="c10">The four ΔG cases</h2>
  <div class="tbl"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">ΔH</th><th scope="col">ΔS</th><th scope="col">Low T</th><th scope="col">High T</th><th scope="col">Driven by</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>−</td><td>+</td><td>Spontaneous</td><td>Spontaneous</td><td>Both enthalpy and entropy</td></tr>
      <tr><td>+</td><td>−</td><td>Nonspontaneous</td><td>Nonspontaneous</td><td>Neither</td></tr>
      <tr><td>−</td><td>−</td><td>Spontaneous</td><td>Nonspontaneous</td><td>Enthalpy (wins at low T)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>+</td><td>+</td><td>Nonspontaneous</td><td>Spontaneous</td><td>Entropy (wins at high T)</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row" colspan="2">Crossover</th><td colspan="3">T = ΔH/ΔS, only for the two same-sign cases</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div></section>

  <section class="flow" aria-labelledby="c11"><h2 id="c11">Spontaneous vs fast</h2>
  <div class="tbl"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Thermodynamics</th><th scope="col">Kinetics</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Question</th><td>Can it happen? Which direction?</td><td>How fast?</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Key quantity</th><td>ΔG (state function)</td><td>Activation energy, rate constant</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Depends on path?</th><td>No</td><td>Yes (mechanism, catalyst)</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Example</th><td colspan="2">Diamond → graphite and the rusting of a nail are both spontaneous but slow. A catalyst speeds a reaction without changing ΔG.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div></section>

  <section class="flow" aria-labelledby="c12"><h2 id="c12">Predicting the sign of ΔS</h2>
  <div class="tbl"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Change</th><th scope="col">ΔS</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Solid → liquid → gas</td><td><span class="sign p">+</span></td><td>H<sub>2</sub>O(l) → H<sub>2</sub>O(g): +109 J/mol·K</td></tr>
      <tr><td>More moles of gas</td><td><span class="sign p">+</span></td><td>2H<sub>2</sub>O(l) → 2H<sub>2</sub>(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g): +326.4 J/K</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fewer moles of gas</td><td><span class="sign n">−</span></td><td>2H<sub>2</sub>(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) → 2H<sub>2</sub>O(g): −88.8 J/K</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Dissolving a solid</td><td><span class="sign p">+</span> (usually)</td><td>NaCl(s) → Na<sup>+</sup>(aq) + Cl<sup>−</sup>(aq)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Heating a substance</td><td><span class="sign p">+</span></td><td>More thermal motion</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Precipitation, freezing, condensation</td><td><span class="sign n">−</span></td><td>Ag<sup>+</sup>(aq) + Cl<sup>−</sup>(aq) → AgCl(s)</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div></section>
  <h2 style="margin-top:12px">Chemical equilibrium</h2>
  <section class="flow" aria-labelledby="ca1"><h2 id="ca1">K<sub>c</sub> vs K<sub>p</sub></h2>
  <div class="tbl"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">K<sub>c</sub></th><th scope="col">K<sub>p</sub></th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Uses</th><td>Molar concentrations [X]</td><td>Partial pressures P<sub>X</sub> in <b>atm</b></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">For</th><td>Solutions and gases</td><td>Gases only</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Conversion</th><td colspan="2">K<sub>p</sub> = K<sub>c</sub>(RT)<sup>Δn</sup>, R = 0.0821 L·atm/mol·K, Δn = gas moles of products − reactants</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Equal when</th><td colspan="2">Δn = 0 (e.g. H<sub>2</sub> + I<sub>2</sub> ⇌ 2HI)</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Haber example</th><td class="num">3.5 × 10⁸</td><td class="num">5.8 × 10⁵ (Δn = −2)</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div></section>

  <section class="flow" aria-labelledby="ca2"><h2 id="ca2">Homogeneous vs heterogeneous equilibrium</h2>
  <div class="tbl"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Homogeneous</th><th scope="col">Heterogeneous</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Phases</th><td>All species in one phase</td><td>More than one phase</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Example</th><td>N<sub>2</sub>(g) + 3H<sub>2</sub>(g) ⇌ 2NH<sub>3</sub>(g)</td><td>FeO(s) + CO(g) ⇌ Fe(s) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g)</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">K expression</th><td>[NH<sub>3</sub>]<sup>2</sup>/[N<sub>2</sub>][H<sub>2</sub>]<sup>3</sup></td><td>K<sub>p</sub> = P<sub>CO<sub>2</sub></sub>/P<sub>CO</sub> (solids omitted)</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Ionic examples</th><td>Weak acid ionization in water (liquid water omitted)</td><td>K<sub>sp</sub>: AgCl(s) ⇌ Ag<sup>+</sup> + Cl<sup>−</sup></td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div></section>

  <section class="flow" aria-labelledby="ca3"><h2 id="ca3">K vs Q</h2>
  <div class="tbl"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">K (equilibrium constant)</th><th scope="col">Q (reaction quotient)</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Concentrations used</th><td>At equilibrium only</td><td>At any moment</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Changes with</th><td>Temperature only</td><td>Every addition, removal, dilution</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Q &lt; K</th><td colspan="2">Too few products: reaction proceeds forward</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Q &gt; K</th><td colspan="2">Too many products: reaction proceeds in reverse (for K<sub>sp</sub>: precipitate forms)</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Q = K</th><td colspan="2">At equilibrium (for K<sub>sp</sub>: saturated)</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div></section>

  <section class="flow" aria-labelledby="ca4"><h2 id="ca4">Le Châtelier: what moves, what doesn't</h2>
  <div class="tbl"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Stress</th><th scope="col">Position shifts?</th><th scope="col">K changes?</th><th scope="col">Rate to reach equilibrium</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Concentration</th><td>Yes, away from what was added</td><td>No</td><td>—</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Volume / pressure</th><td>Yes, if Δn<sub>gas</sub> ≠ 0</td><td>No</td><td>—</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Inert gas, constant V</th><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>—</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Temperature</th><td>Yes, toward the endothermic side when heated</td><td><b>Yes</b></td><td>Faster when hotter</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Catalyst</th><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Faster</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div></section>

  <section class="flow" aria-labelledby="ca5"><h2 id="ca5">Equal rates vs equal concentrations</h2>
  <div class="tbl"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">At equilibrium…</th><th scope="col">True?</th><th scope="col">Why</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Forward rate = reverse rate</td><td><b>Always</b></td><td>That is the definition of dynamic equilibrium</td></tr>
      <tr><td>[reactants] = [products]</td><td>Usually not</td><td>The ratio is set by K (N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub> 0.75 M vs NO<sub>2</sub> 0.50 M)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Concentrations are constant</td><td><b>Always</b></td><td>Formation and consumption cancel</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Reactions have stopped</td><td>Never</td><td>Both directions continue (dynamic)</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div></section>

</section>

