  <section class="flow" aria-labelledby="r-mn"><h2 id="r-mn">Memory hooks</h2>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card mn"><h4>Sign convention</h4><div class="big">"Into the system is plus"</div><p>Heat in, work done on the system, energy gained: all <b>+</b>. Anything leaving the system (heat released, work done by it) is <b>−</b>. Think of the system's bank account.</p></div>
    <div class="card mn"><h4>First law</h4><div class="big">ΔE = q + w</div><p>"Change = heat + work." Deposits and withdrawals of two currencies into one account.</p></div>
    <div class="card mn"><h4>Which calorimeter measures what</h4><div class="big">Bomb → E · Cup → H</div><p>The <b>b</b>omb is <b>b</b>oxed in (constant V, ΔE). The <b>c</b>up is open to the air (<b>c</b>onstant P, ΔH).</p></div>
    <div class="card mn"><h4>ΔE ↔ ΔH</h4><div class="big">ΔH = ΔE + Δn<sub>g</sub>RT</div><p>"Gases only, products minus reactants." Count solids and liquids as zero. If gas moles shrink, ΔH is more negative than ΔE.</p></div>
    <div class="card mn"><h4>State vs path</h4><div class="big">"Capitals keep, lowercase leak"</div><p>Capital letters (E, H, S, G, P, V, T) are state functions that keep only the endpoints. Lowercase (q, w) depend on the route.</p></div>
    <div class="card mn"><h4>Hess's law</h4><div class="big">Flip → flip the sign · Scale → scale ΔH</div><p>Then cancel anything on both sides and add.</p></div>
    <div class="card mn"><h4>Bond enthalpy</h4><div class="big">"Break in, Make out" (B − M)</div><p>Breaking bonds takes energy in (+); making bonds gives energy out (−). ΔH ≈ Σbroken − Σformed.</p></div>
    <div class="card mn"><h4>Born–Haber</h4><div class="big">"S-I-D-E-U"</div><p><b>S</b>ublimation + <b>I</b>onization + <b>D</b>issociation + <b>E</b>lectron affinity + lattice energy <b>U</b> = ΔH°<sub>f</sub>.</p></div>
    <div class="card mn"><h4>Phase-change enthalpy</h4><div class="big">sub = fus + vap</div><p>Solid to gas directly equals melting then boiling (Hess's law).</p></div>
    <div class="card mn"><h4>Entropy goes up when…</h4><div class="big">"Gas, Heat, Split, Dissolve"</div><p>More <b>gas</b> moles, higher <b>T</b> (heat), molecules <b>split</b> into more particles, solids <b>dissolve</b>.</p></div>
    <div class="card mn"><h4>Gibbs</h4><div class="big">"Good Houses Take Shape"</div><p><b>G</b> = <b>H</b> − <b>T</b><b>S</b>. Negative ΔG = "go." Use kelvin and match kJ with kJ.</p></div>
    <div class="card mn"><h4>Four cases</h4><div class="big">"Same signs, temperature decides"</div><p>Opposite signs: the answer never changes (−,+ always yes; +,− never). Same signs: crossover at T = ΔH/ΔS; negative ΔS → spontaneous at low T, positive ΔS → at high T.</p></div>
    <div class="card mn"><h4>Writing K</h4><div class="big">"Products over reactants; solids and liquids sit out"</div><p>Raise each concentration to its coefficient. Pure solids and liquids (including water as solvent) never appear in K.</p></div>
    <div class="card mn"><h4>K<sub>c</sub> ↔ K<sub>p</sub></h4><div class="big">K<sub>p</sub> = K<sub>c</sub>(RT)<sup>Δn</sup></div><p>Δn counts <b>gas</b> moles only, products minus reactants. R = 0.0821. If Δn = 0, they are equal.</p></div>
    <div class="card mn"><h4>Manipulating K</h4><div class="big">Flip → 1/K · ×n → K<sup>n</sup> · Add → multiply</div><p>Because ΔG° = −RT ln K: flipping negates ΔG°, scaling scales it, and adding ΔG° values multiplies the Ks.</p></div>
    <div class="card mn"><h4>ΔG° and K</h4><div class="big">"Negative G, big K"</div><p>ΔG° &lt; 0 ↔ K &gt; 1 (products). ΔG° &gt; 0 ↔ K &lt; 1. About 5.7 kJ/mol of ΔG° = a factor of 10 in K at 25 °C.</p></div>
    <div class="card mn"><h4>Le Châtelier</h4><div class="big">"Push it, it pushes back"</div><p>Add → shift away. Remove → shift toward. Squeeze → fewer gas moles. Heat → endothermic side. <b>Only temperature changes K.</b></p></div>
    <div class="card mn"><h4>Cobalt chloride</h4><div class="big">"Blue when hot and salty, pink when wet and cold"</div><p>[CoCl<sub>4</sub>]<sup>2−</sup> (blue) is favored by heat and Cl<sup>−</sup>; [Co(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>2+</sup> (pink) by water and cooling. Used in humidity indicators.</p></div>
  </div></section>

