  <!-- connection chain -->
  <section class="bench" aria-labelledby="v-chain">
    <div class="bench-head"><h2 id="v-chain">How the whole chapter connects</h2><span class="hint">Select a box to open its lesson</span></div>
    <ol class="chain">
      <li><a href="#d-first"><b>q + w</b><span>energy crosses the boundary</span></a></li>
      <li><a href="#d-first"><b>ΔE</b><span>first law: ΔE = q + w</span></a></li>
      <li><a href="#d-enth"><b>ΔH</b><span>= q at constant P</span></a></li>
      <li><a href="#d-spont"><b>ΔS<sub>surr</sub></b><span>= −ΔH/T</span></a></li>
      <li><a href="#d-ent"><b>ΔS<sub>univ</sub></b><span>= ΔS<sub>sys</sub> + ΔS<sub>surr</sub> &gt; 0</span></a></li>
      <li><a href="#d-gibbs"><b>ΔG</b><span>= −TΔS<sub>univ</sub> = ΔH − TΔS</span></a></li>
      <li><a href="#v-gx"><b>K</b><span>ΔG° = −RT ln K</span></a></li>
    </ol>
    <p class="why"><b>Read it left to right:</b> heat released by the system (ΔH &lt; 0) is entropy gained by the surroundings. Add that to the system's own entropy change and you have the second law. Multiply by −T and you have ΔG, which is why "ΔG &lt; 0" and "ΔS<sub>univ</sub> &gt; 0" are the same statement. ΔG° then sets K, which is where chemical equilibrium begins.</p>
