  <!-- V9 Trouton -->
  <section class="bench" aria-labelledby="v-tr">
    <div class="bench-head"><h2 id="v-tr">9 · Analysis: Trouton's rule and hydrogen bonding</h2><span class="hint">Lessons 9 and 11</span></div>
    <p style="max-width:68ch">At the normal boiling point, ΔG<sub>vap</sub> = 0, so ΔS<sub>vap</sub> = ΔH<sub>vap</sub>/T<sub>b</sub>. Trouton noticed this ratio is about 88 J/mol·K for many liquids: the liquid-to-gas jump in disorder is roughly the same. The points that sit above the line are the ones whose liquids are <i>more ordered</i> than usual.</p>
    <div class="cwrap"><svg class="chart" id="trC" viewBox="0 0 640 340" role="img" aria-labelledby="trT trD"><title id="trT">ΔH of vaporization versus normal boiling point for fourteen liquids</title><desc id="trD">Most liquids lie near a line of slope 88 J/mol·K through the origin. Water, ethanol, methanol and ammonia lie above it.</desc></svg></div>
    <div class="legend-row"><span><i style="background:var(--cold)"></i>no hydrogen bonding</span><span><i style="background:var(--hot)"></i>hydrogen-bonded liquid</span><span><i style="background:var(--muted)"></i>Trouton line, 88 J/mol·K</span></div>
    <details class="worked"><summary>Data table</summary><div><div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Liquid</th><th class="num">T<sub>b</sub> (K)</th><th class="num">ΔH<sub>vap</sub> (kJ/mol)</th><th class="num">ΔS<sub>vap</sub> (J/mol·K)</th></tr></thead><tbody id="trB"></tbody></table></div></div></details>
    <p class="why"><b>Correlate:</b> water's ΔS<sub>vap</sub> (109) is high because hydrogen bonds already hold liquid water in a partly ordered network, so escaping to gas gains extra freedom. Use Trouton's rule to <b>estimate</b>: a non-H-bonded liquid boiling at 350 K should have ΔH<sub>vap</sub> ≈ 0.088 × 350 ≈ 31 kJ/mol (benzene: 30.8).</p>
  </section>

