const TOPICS={sys:"Systems & state functions",first:"First law",work:"Work & processes",enth:"Enthalpy",cal:"Calorimetry",form:"Heats of formation",hess:"Hess's law",bond:"Bond enthalpy & Born–Haber",phase:"Phase changes",spont:"Spontaneity & 2nd law",ent:"Entropy & 3rd law",gibbs:"Gibbs free energy",eqk:"Equilibrium & K",lech:"Le Châtelier",gk:"ΔG° & K"};
const C=(k,q,o,a,e)=>({t:"c",k,q,o,a,e}), S=(k,q,o,a,e)=>({t:"s",k,q,o,a,e}), I=(k,q,a,show,e)=>({t:"i",k,q,a,show,e});
const NEW_CE=[
C("eqk","At chemical equilibrium, which statement is <b>always</b> true?",["The concentrations of reactants and products are equal","The forward and reverse reaction rates are equal","Both reactions have stopped","K equals 1"],1,"<b>The forward and reverse rates are equal.</b> That is the definition of dynamic equilibrium: both reactions continue, but they cancel, so concentrations stay constant. The concentrations themselves are usually not equal (N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub> 0.75 M vs NO<sub>2</sub> 0.50 M in your handout)."),
C("eqk","Chemical equilibrium can only be established in:",["An open system","A closed system","An isolated system only","Any system, once T is constant"],1,"<b>A closed system</b>, where nothing is added or removed. In an open flask, a gaseous product escapes and the reverse reaction can't keep up, so the reaction is pulled to completion instead of reaching equilibrium."),
C("eqk","What is the correct equilibrium expression for CaCO<sub>3</sub>(s) ⇌ CaO(s) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g)?",["K<sub>p</sub> = P<sub>CO<sub>2</sub></sub>","K<sub>c</sub> = [CaO][CO<sub>2</sub>]/[CaCO<sub>3</sub>]","K<sub>p</sub> = P<sub>CO<sub>2</sub></sub>/P<sub>CaCO<sub>3</sub></sub>","K = 1"],0,"<b>K<sub>p</sub> = P<sub>CO<sub>2</sub></sub>.</b> This is a heterogeneous equilibrium. Pure solids have constant \"concentrations\" and are left out of K, so only the gas appears. That means the CO<sub>2</sub> pressure above limestone at a given temperature is fixed, no matter how much solid is present."),
C("eqk","When is K<sub>c</sub> equal to K<sub>p</sub>?",["Always","When Δn of gas = 0","When T = 273 K","When K = 1"],1,"<b>When Δn (gas) = 0.</b> K<sub>p</sub> = K<sub>c</sub>(RT)<sup>Δn</sup>, and anything to the power 0 is 1. Example: H<sub>2</sub>(g) + I<sub>2</sub>(g) ⇌ 2HI(g)."),
S("eqk","The equilibrium constant for A ⇌ B is 4.0. What is K for 2B ⇌ 2A?",["16","0.25","0.0625","−8.0"],2,"<b>0.0625 (= 1/16).</b> Reversing gives 1/4.0 = 0.25; doubling the coefficients squares it: 0.25<sup>2</sup> = 0.0625. Because ΔG° = −RT ln K, reversing negates ΔG° and doubling doubles it."),
S("eqk","For 2NO(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) ⇌ 2NO<sub>2</sub>(g), K<sub>c</sub> = 6.9 × 10<sup>5</sup> at 500 K. What is K<sub>p</sub>? (R = 0.0821)",["2.8 × 10<sup>7</sup>","6.9 × 10<sup>5</sup>","1.7 × 10<sup>4</sup>","41"],2,"<b>1.7 × 10<sup>4</sup>.</b> Δn = 2 − 3 = −1, so K<sub>p</sub> = K<sub>c</sub>(RT)<sup>−1</sup> = 6.9 × 10<sup>5</sup>/(0.0821 × 500) = 6.9 × 10<sup>5</sup>/41.05 = 1.7 × 10<sup>4</sup>. Multiplying instead of dividing gives 2.8 × 10<sup>7</sup>."),
S("eqk","At equilibrium, a flask contains 0.75 M N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub> and 0.50 M NO<sub>2</sub> (N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub> ⇌ 2NO<sub>2</sub>). What is K<sub>c</sub>?",["0.67","0.33","1.5","3.0"],1,"<b>0.33.</b> K<sub>c</sub> = [NO<sub>2</sub>]<sup>2</sup>/[N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>4</sub>] = (0.50)<sup>2</sup>/0.75 = 0.25/0.75 = 0.33. Forgetting to square [NO<sub>2</sub>] gives 0.67."),
S("eqk","For H<sub>2</sub> + I<sub>2</sub> ⇌ 2HI, K<sub>c</sub> = 50.5. A mixture has [H<sub>2</sub>] = [I<sub>2</sub>] = 0.10 M and [HI] = 0.50 M. What happens?",["Q = 25 &lt; K, so the reaction proceeds forward","Q = 25 &lt; K, so the reaction proceeds in reverse","Q = 50 = K, so it is at equilibrium","Q = 100 &gt; K, so the reaction proceeds in reverse"],0,"<b>Q = 25 &lt; K, so it proceeds forward.</b> Q = (0.50)<sup>2</sup>/[(0.10)(0.10)] = 25. Since Q is smaller than K, there is too little product; HI forms until Q rises to 50.5."),
S("eqk","H<sub>2</sub> + I<sub>2</sub> ⇌ 2HI, K<sub>c</sub> = 50.5. Starting with 1.00 M H<sub>2</sub> and 1.00 M I<sub>2</sub>, what is [HI] at equilibrium?",["0.78 M","1.56 M","7.11 M","0.22 M"],1,"<b>1.56 M.</b> ICE: (2x)<sup>2</sup>/(1.00 − x)<sup>2</sup> = 50.5. Square-root both sides: 2x/(1.00 − x) = 7.11, so x = 0.780 and [HI] = 2x = 1.56 M. 0.78 M is x itself, and 0.22 M is the leftover H<sub>2</sub>."),
I("eqk","What is the law that states K equals the product concentrations raised to their coefficients, divided by the reactant concentrations raised to theirs, called?",["law of mass action","mass action","law of mass-action"],"Law of mass action","<b>Law of mass action.</b> It gives the form of every equilibrium expression: K<sub>c</sub> = [C]<sup>c</sup>[D]<sup>d</sup>/[A]<sup>a</sup>[B]<sup>b</sup>. Pure solids and liquids are left out."),
I("eqk","What do you call an equilibrium in which the reactants and products are present in more than one phase, such as a solid in equilibrium with a gas?",["heterogeneous","heterogeneous equilibrium"],"Heterogeneous equilibrium","<b>Heterogeneous equilibrium</b>, e.g. FeO(s) + CO(g) ⇌ Fe(s) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g) or any K<sub>sp</sub> equilibrium. Solids and pure liquids drop out of K. When everything is in one phase, it is homogeneous."),
C("lech","For N<sub>2</sub>(g) + 3H<sub>2</sub>(g) ⇌ 2NH<sub>3</sub>(g), ΔH = −92 kJ, which change <b>increases</b> the equilibrium amount of NH<sub>3</sub>?",["Raising the temperature","Decreasing the volume (raising pressure)","Adding a catalyst","Adding argon at constant volume"],1,"<b>Decreasing the volume.</b> Higher pressure favors the side with fewer gas moles (2 NH<sub>3</sub> vs 4 reactant molecules). Raising T favors the endothermic reverse reaction; a catalyst and an inert gas at constant volume don't shift the equilibrium."),
C("lech","An inert gas (argon) is added to a gaseous equilibrium at constant volume. What happens?",["Shifts toward fewer moles of gas","Shifts toward more moles of gas","No shift","K increases"],2,"<b>No shift.</b> Total pressure rises, but the partial pressures (and concentrations) of the reacting gases are unchanged, so Q still equals K. Only at constant pressure, where the volume expands, would it shift toward more gas moles."),
C("lech","Which stress changes the <b>value</b> of K?",["Adding a reactant","Changing the volume","Changing the temperature","Adding a catalyst"],2,"<b>Changing the temperature.</b> K = k<sub>f</sub>/k<sub>r</sub>, and the two rate constants respond differently to temperature. Concentration and pressure changes only make Q differ from K temporarily; the system shifts until Q = K again with the same K."),
C("lech","What does adding a catalyst to a system at equilibrium do?",["Shifts it toward products","Shifts it toward reactants","Nothing to the position; it only helps reach equilibrium faster","Increases K"],2,"<b>No change in position.</b> A catalyst lowers the activation energy of the forward and reverse reactions equally, so both speed up by the same factor. Equilibrium is reached faster, but the composition at equilibrium is unchanged."),
C("lech","In the cobalt chloride equilibrium [Co(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>2+</sup> (pink) + 4Cl<sup>−</sup> ⇌ [CoCl<sub>4</sub>]<sup>2−</sup> (blue) + 6H<sub>2</sub>O, AgNO<sub>3</sub> is added to a blue solution. What is observed?",["It turns deeper blue","It turns pink as AgCl precipitates","No change","It turns colorless"],1,"<b>It turns pink.</b> Ag<sup>+</sup> removes Cl<sup>−</sup> as solid AgCl. Removing a reactant shifts the equilibrium to the left, back toward pink [Co(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>2+</sup>."),
C("lech","2CrO<sub>4</sub><sup>2−</sup> (yellow) + 2H<sup>+</sup> ⇌ Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub><sup>2−</sup> (orange) + H<sub>2</sub>O. NaOH is added to an orange solution. What happens?",["It turns more orange","It turns yellow","Nothing","A precipitate forms"],1,"<b>It turns yellow.</b> OH<sup>−</sup> neutralizes H<sup>+</sup>, removing a reactant, so the equilibrium shifts left toward yellow chromate. Adding acid does the opposite."),
C("lech","An exothermic reaction at equilibrium is heated. What happens to K?",["K increases","K decreases","K is unchanged","K becomes 1"],1,"<b>K decreases.</b> For an exothermic reaction, heat is a product. Raising T shifts the equilibrium toward reactants, and because temperature changes K itself, the new equilibrium has a smaller K."),
I("lech","What principle states that a system at equilibrium subjected to a stress shifts in the direction that relieves the stress?",["le chatelier","le chateliers principle","le chatelier principle","le chateliers","lechatelier","le chatelier's principle"],"Le Châtelier's principle","<b>Le Châtelier's principle</b> (Henri Le Châtelier, 1884). It's a shortcut for comparing Q with K: any stress that makes Q ≠ K is undone by a shift that restores Q = K."),
S("gk","A reaction has ΔG° = −10.0 kJ/mol at 298 K. What is K?",["0.018","4.0","57","1.0 × 10<sup>10</sup>"],2,"<b>About 57.</b> K = e<sup>−ΔG°/RT</sup> = e<sup>10 000/(8.314 × 298)</sup> = e<sup>4.04</sup> = 57. Negative ΔG° gives K &gt; 1. Using +10.0 kJ gives 0.018, the reciprocal."),
S("gk","A reaction has K = 1.0 × 10<sup>−3</sup> at 298 K. What is ΔG°?",["−17.1 kJ/mol","+17.1 kJ/mol","+7.4 kJ/mol","+2.5 kJ/mol"],1,"<b>+17.1 kJ/mol.</b> ΔG° = −RT ln K = −(8.314)(298) ln(1.0 × 10<sup>−3</sup>) = −(2478)(−6.91) = +17 100 J/mol. K &lt; 1 means reactants are favored, so ΔG° is positive. Using log instead of ln gives +7.4 kJ."),
C("gk","An equilibrium constant greater than 1 corresponds to:",["ΔG° &gt; 0, reactants favored","ΔG° &lt; 0, products favored","ΔG° = 0","ΔG° that depends only on rate"],1,"<b>ΔG° &lt; 0, products favored.</b> From ΔG° = −RT ln K, K &gt; 1 makes ln K positive and ΔG° negative. K tells you which side is favored at equilibrium; the sign of ΔG° tells you which way the reaction tends to run from standard conditions."),
];
const QUIZ=[
// systems
C("sys","A sealed, stoppered flask in which a reaction warms the glass is what type of system?",["Open","Closed","Isolated","Adiabatic"],1,"<b>Closed.</b> The stopper keeps matter in, but heat still passes through the glass. Energy without matter = closed. An isolated system would need insulation as well."),
C("sys","Which property is <b>intensive</b>?",["Volume","Mass","Enthalpy","Boiling point"],3,"<b>Boiling point.</b> It is the same for a drop or a drum of water, so it does not depend on amount. Volume, mass and enthalpy all double when you double the sample (extensive)."),
C("sys","Which of these is a <b>path</b> function?",["Internal energy, E","Enthalpy, H","Work, w","Entropy, S"],2,"<b>Work.</b> How much work a process does depends on how it is carried out (e.g. reversible vs irreversible expansion between the same two states). E, H and S depend only on the state, like altitude on a mountain."),
C("sys","In ΔE = q + w, which statement is correct?",["All three are state functions","ΔE is a state function, while q and w are path functions","q and w are state functions, ΔE is not","None of them are state functions"],1,"<b>ΔE is a state function; q and w are path functions.</b> Different routes split the energy differently between heat and work, but their sum is fixed by the initial and final states. The handout's letter clue: capital E, lowercase q and w."),
I("sys","What is the part of the universe under investigation, separated from the rest by a real or imaginary boundary, called?",["system","the system"],"System","<b>System.</b> Everything outside it is the surroundings, and together they make the universe. Choosing the system sets every sign in the first law, because q and w are measured from the system's point of view."),
I("sys","What type of system exchanges neither matter nor energy with its surroundings?",["isolated","isolated system"],"Isolated system","<b>Isolated system.</b> Open exchanges both; closed exchanges energy only. A sealed, insulated bomb calorimeter approximates an isolated system, which is why the heat released by the reaction equals the heat gained by the calorimeter."),
I("sys","What do you call a property, such as P, V, T, H or S, that depends only on the initial and final states?",["state function","state functions","state property","state variable"],"State function","<b>State function.</b> Like a change in altitude, its change doesn't depend on the route. That is why Hess's law and Born–Haber cycles let you calculate ΔH by any convenient path."),
// first law
C("first","Under the sign convention in your handout, w is positive when:",["The system does work on the surroundings","Work is done on the system","The system expands","Heat is released"],1,"<b>Work is done on the system.</b> Energy entering the system is positive. Compression pushes energy into the gas, so w &gt; 0. Expansion means the system pushes on the surroundings, so w &lt; 0."),
C("first","A change in the internal energy of the system is always:",["Equal in sign to the change in the surroundings","Opposite in sign and equal in size to the change in the surroundings","Zero for any chemical reaction","Larger than the change in the surroundings"],1,"<b>Opposite in sign, equal in size.</b> The first law says energy is conserved: what the system gains, the surroundings lose exactly (the handout's paired bar graphs). ΔE<sub>sys</sub> = −ΔE<sub>surr</sub>."),
C("first","In an adiabatic process:",["ΔE = q","ΔE = w","ΔE = 0","w = 0"],1,"<b>ΔE = w.</b> Adiabatic means no heat flows (q = 0), so ΔE = 0 + w. When a gas expands adiabatically, the work comes entirely out of its internal energy, so it cools."),
C("first","Ice melting in a glass at room temperature with no work involved has:",["q &lt; 0, ΔE &lt; 0, exothermic","q &gt; 0, ΔE &gt; 0, endothermic","q = 0, ΔE = 0","q &gt; 0, ΔE &lt; 0"],1,"<b>q &gt; 0, ΔE &gt; 0, endothermic.</b> With w = 0, ΔE = q. The ice absorbs heat from the surroundings to break its hydrogen-bond network, so heat flows in and the system's energy rises."),
S("first","A system absorbs 125 J of heat and the surroundings do 85 J of work on it. What is ΔE?",["−210 J","+40 J","−40 J","+210 J"],3,"<b>+210 J.</b> Heat absorbed: q = +125 J. Work done on the system: w = +85 J. ΔE = 125 + 85 = +210 J. Both flows enter the system, so both are positive."),
S("first","A gas absorbs 1.2 kJ of heat while expanding and doing 3.4 kJ of work. What is ΔE of the gas?",["+4.6 kJ","+2.2 kJ","−2.2 kJ","−4.6 kJ"],2,"<b>−2.2 kJ.</b> q = +1.2 kJ, w = −3.4 kJ (work done by the gas). ΔE = 1.2 − 3.4 = −2.2 kJ. It paid out more as work than it took in as heat."),
S("first","A system's internal energy falls by 450 J while it releases 300 J of heat. What is w?",["−150 J","+150 J","−750 J","+750 J"],0,"<b>w = −150 J</b> (work done by the system). Rearrange ΔE = q + w: w = ΔE − q = −450 − (−300) = −150 J. Of the 450 J lost, 300 J left as heat and 150 J as work."),
S("first","In an adiabatic compression, 500 J of work is done on a gas. What is ΔE?",["0 J","−500 J","+500 J","+1000 J"],2,"<b>+500 J.</b> Adiabatic: q = 0. Work on the gas: w = +500 J. ΔE = +500 J, so the gas warms up. This is why a bicycle pump gets hot."),
I("first","What is the law that says energy is neither created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction, ΔE = q + w, called?",["first law","first law of thermodynamics","law of conservation of energy","conservation of energy","1st law","1st law of thermodynamics"],"First law of thermodynamics","<b>First law of thermodynamics</b> (conservation of energy). It does the bookkeeping: any change in the system's energy is exactly accounted for by heat and work crossing the boundary."),
I("first","What is the sum of the kinetic and potential energies of all the particles in a system called?",["internal energy","e","u","internal energy e","internal energy u"],"Internal energy (E or U)","<b>Internal energy, E</b> (some books write U). Its absolute value can't be measured, only its change, ΔE, which is why every thermochemistry problem is about changes."),
I("first","What do you call a process in which no heat flows between system and surroundings (q = 0)?",["adiabatic","adiabatic process"],"Adiabatic","<b>Adiabatic.</b> With q = 0, ΔE = w. In the lab it is approximated with strong insulation or by working so fast that heat has no time to flow."),
// work
C("work","In w = −PΔV for an expansion against the atmosphere, P is:",["The pressure of the gas inside","The external (opposing) pressure","The average of the two","Always 1 atm by definition"],1,"<b>The external pressure.</b> Work is done against whatever opposes the expansion. The handout stresses this: P refers to the outside pressure, not the pressure inside the chamber."),
C("work","Why does a reversible expansion do more work than an irreversible one between the same states?",["It is faster","It pushes against the maximum possible opposing pressure at every instant (P<sub>ext</sub> = P<sub>gas</sub>)","Its ΔE is larger","It absorbs no heat"],1,"<b>It pushes against the largest pressure it can still overcome.</b> On a P–V diagram, work is the area under the path, and the reversible isotherm encloses more area than the flat line at a fixed, lower P<sub>ext</sub>. Reversible work is the maximum obtainable."),
C("work","For the isothermal expansion of an ideal gas:",["ΔE = q","ΔE = 0 and ΔH = 0, so q = −w","w = 0","q = 0"],1,"<b>ΔE = 0 and ΔH = 0, so q = −w.</b> An ideal gas's internal energy depends only on T. Same T means same E, so every joule of heat absorbed leaves as work."),
C("work","For an ideal gas, C<sub>p</sub> − C<sub>v</sub> equals:",["0","R","2R","RT"],1,"<b>R.</b> At constant pressure, heating must raise the temperature (nC<sub>v</sub>ΔT) and also pay for expansion work (nRΔT). So nC<sub>p</sub>ΔT = nC<sub>v</sub>ΔT + nRΔT and C<sub>p</sub> = C<sub>v</sub> + R."),
S("work","A gas expands from 3.0 L to 8.0 L against a constant external pressure of 1.2 atm. What is w? (1 L·atm = 101.3 J)",["+608 J","−608 J","−6.0 J","−405 J"],1,"<b>−608 J.</b> w = −(1.2 atm)(5.0 L) = −6.0 L·atm × 101.3 J/L·atm = −608 J. Negative because the gas does work on the surroundings. Forgetting the conversion leaves you with −6.0 L·atm."),
S("work","A gas is compressed from 10.0 L to 4.0 L by a constant external pressure of 2.0 atm. What is w?",["−1.22 kJ","+1.22 kJ","+12 J","−12 J"],1,"<b>+1.22 kJ.</b> ΔV = 4.0 − 10.0 = −6.0 L. w = −(2.0)(−6.0) = +12 L·atm = +1216 J. Positive because the surroundings did work on the gas."),
S("work","2.00 mol of ideal gas expands reversibly and isothermally at 300 K from 5.0 L to 20.0 L. What is w?",["−6.92 kJ","−3.46 kJ","+6.92 kJ","−15.0 kJ"],0,"<b>−6.92 kJ.</b> w = −nRT ln(V<sub>2</sub>/V<sub>1</sub>) = −(2.00)(8.314)(300) ln 4 = −4988 × 1.386 = −6915 J. Using log instead of ln, or forgetting n, are the usual slips."),
S("work","A gas expands into a vacuum (P<sub>ext</sub> = 0). What is w?",["−nRT","0","+PΔV","Cannot be found"],1,"<b>0.</b> w = −P<sub>ext</sub>ΔV, and with nothing to push against, P<sub>ext</sub> = 0. This \"free expansion\" does no work even though the volume increases."),
S("work","An ideal gas expands isothermally and absorbs 820 J of heat. What is w?",["+820 J","0","−820 J","−1640 J"],2,"<b>−820 J.</b> Isothermal ideal gas: ΔE = 0, so w = −q = −820 J. All the absorbed heat leaves as work done on the surroundings."),
I("work","Name the process carried out at constant pressure.",["isobaric","isobaric process"],"Isobaric","<b>Isobaric.</b> Here q<sub>p</sub> = ΔH = nC<sub>p</sub>ΔT. Constant volume is isochoric; constant temperature is isothermal."),
I("work","Name the process carried out at constant volume, where w = 0 and q<sub>v</sub> = ΔE.",["isochoric","isochoric process","isovolumetric","isometric"],"Isochoric","<b>Isochoric</b> (also isovolumetric). No volume change means no PΔV work, so all heat goes into internal energy. A bomb calorimeter runs isochorically."),
// enthalpy
C("enth","A coffee-cup reaction absorbs heat from the water, and the temperature drops. The reaction is:",["Exothermic, ΔH &lt; 0","Endothermic, ΔH &gt; 0","Neither","Exothermic, ΔH &gt; 0"],1,"<b>Endothermic, ΔH &gt; 0.</b> The reaction (system) took heat from the water (surroundings), so the water cooled. A temperature drop in the surroundings signals an endothermic system, like the NH<sub>4</sub>NO<sub>3</sub> in an instant cold pack."),
C("enth","For which reaction is ΔH = ΔE?",["2H<sub>2</sub>(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) → 2H<sub>2</sub>O(l)","H<sub>2</sub>(g) + Cl<sub>2</sub>(g) → 2HCl(g)","CaCO<sub>3</sub>(s) → CaO(s) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g)","N<sub>2</sub>(g) + 3H<sub>2</sub>(g) → 2NH<sub>3</sub>(g)"],1,"<b>H<sub>2</sub> + Cl<sub>2</sub> → 2HCl.</b> Two moles of gas become two, so Δn<sub>g</sub> = 0 and ΔH = ΔE + 0. The other three change their gas moles."),
C("enth","If ΔH for A → B is −120 kJ, then ΔH for 2B → 2A is:",["−240 kJ","+120 kJ","+240 kJ","−120 kJ"],2,"<b>+240 kJ.</b> Reversing flips the sign (+120) and doubling the coefficients doubles it (+240). Enthalpy is extensive and a state function."),
C("enth","Enthalpy equals the heat exchanged in a process carried out at constant:",["Volume","Temperature","Pressure","Entropy"],2,"<b>Pressure.</b> At constant P, ΔH = ΔE + PΔV = q<sub>p</sub>. The PV term adds back the energy used as expansion work, so the heat measured equals ΔH."),
S("enth","For 2CO(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) → 2CO<sub>2</sub>(g), ΔH = −566.0 kJ at 298 K. What is ΔE?",["−568.5 kJ","−563.5 kJ","−566.0 kJ","−561.0 kJ"],1,"<b>−563.5 kJ.</b> Δn<sub>g</sub> = 2 − 3 = −1. ΔE = ΔH − Δn<sub>g</sub>RT = −566.0 − (−1)(8.314 × 10<sup>−3</sup>)(298) = −566.0 + 2.48 = −563.5 kJ. Gas moles fall, so ΔE is less negative than ΔH."),
S("enth","For CaCO<sub>3</sub>(s) → CaO(s) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g), ΔH = +178.1 kJ at 298 K. What is ΔE?",["+180.6 kJ","+178.1 kJ","+175.6 kJ","+173.1 kJ"],2,"<b>+175.6 kJ.</b> Only CO<sub>2</sub> is a gas: Δn<sub>g</sub> = +1. ΔE = 178.1 − (1)(8.314 × 10<sup>−3</sup>)(298) = 178.1 − 2.48 = 175.6 kJ. Part of the heat absorbed at constant pressure goes into pushing back the atmosphere."),
S("enth","CH<sub>4</sub> combustion has ΔH = −890 kJ/mol. What is q when 8.00 g of CH<sub>4</sub> (16.04 g/mol) burns at constant pressure?",["−890 kJ","−444 kJ","−7120 kJ","+444 kJ"],1,"<b>−444 kJ.</b> n = 8.00/16.04 = 0.499 mol. q = 0.499 × (−890) = −444 kJ. Enthalpy scales with the amount; the sign is negative because heat is released."),
S("enth","2.00 mol of an ideal gas with C<sub>p</sub> = 20.8 J/mol·K is heated by 50.0 K at constant pressure. What is q<sub>p</sub>?",["1040 J","2080 J","4160 J","208 J"],1,"<b>2080 J.</b> q<sub>p</sub> = nC<sub>p</sub>ΔT = (2.00)(20.8)(50.0) = 2080 J, and this also equals ΔH for the process."),
I("enth","What do you call a process that releases heat to the surroundings (ΔH &lt; 0)?",["exothermic","exothermic process","exothermic reaction"],"Exothermic","<b>Exothermic.</b> The system loses heat, so q and ΔH are negative and the surroundings warm up. Combustion and freezing are exothermic."),
I("enth","What is the quantity Δn<sub>g</sub> in ΔH = ΔE + Δn<sub>g</sub>RT?",["moles of gaseous products minus moles of gaseous reactants","change in moles of gas","change in number of moles of gas","moles of gas products minus reactants","gaseous products minus gaseous reactants","change in moles of gas products minus reactants"],"Moles of gaseous products − moles of gaseous reactants","<b>Moles of gaseous products minus moles of gaseous reactants</b>, from the balanced equation. Solids and liquids are left out because their volumes are negligible, so they contribute no PΔV work."),
// calorimetry
C("cal","A bomb calorimeter measures:",["ΔH, because pressure is constant","ΔE, because volume is constant","ΔS","ΔG"],1,"<b>ΔE.</b> The rigid, sealed bomb keeps volume constant, so no PΔV work is possible (w = 0) and q<sub>v</sub> = ΔE. Convert to ΔH afterward with Δn<sub>g</sub>RT."),
C("cal","During a calorimetry run, the calorimeter's temperature rises. Which is true?",["q<sub>rxn</sub> &gt; 0","q<sub>rxn</sub> = −q<sub>cal</sub> &lt; 0; the reaction is exothermic","q<sub>rxn</sub> = q<sub>cal</sub>","No heat was transferred"],1,"<b>q<sub>rxn</sub> = −q<sub>cal</sub>, so the reaction is exothermic.</b> The calorimeter absorbed heat (q<sub>cal</sub> &gt; 0), and by conservation the reaction released exactly that much."),
S("cal","50.0 mL of 1.0 M HCl and 50.0 mL of 1.0 M NaOH are mixed in a coffee cup; T rises 6.8 °C. Taking 100 g of solution with c = 4.18 J/g·°C, what is ΔH per mole of water formed?",["+57 kJ/mol","−2.8 kJ/mol","−57 kJ/mol","−28 kJ/mol"],2,"<b>−57 kJ/mol.</b> q<sub>soln</sub> = (100)(4.18)(6.8) = 2842 J. Moles of H<sub>2</sub>O = 0.050 mol. ΔH = −2.842 kJ/0.050 mol = −57 kJ/mol. Negative because the solution warmed (exothermic). −2.8 kJ is the total heat before dividing by moles."),
S("cal","1.00 g of glucose (180.16 g/mol) burns in a bomb with C<sub>cal</sub> = 5.00 kJ/°C, raising T by 3.13 °C. What is ΔE per mole?",["−15.7 kJ/mol","−2.82 × 10<sup>3</sup> kJ/mol","+2.82 × 10<sup>3</sup> kJ/mol","−563 kJ/mol"],1,"<b>−2.82 × 10<sup>3</sup> kJ/mol.</b> q<sub>v</sub> = −(5.00)(3.13) = −15.65 kJ per gram. × 180.16 g/mol = −2820 kJ/mol. That matches glucose's real energy content, about 3.7 kcal per gram."),
S("cal","25.0 g of a metal at 95.0 °C is dropped into 100.0 g of water at 22.0 °C; the final temperature is 24.2 °C. What is the specific heat of the metal? (c<sub>water</sub> = 4.184 J/g·°C)",["0.130 J/g·°C","0.520 J/g·°C","4.18 J/g·°C","2.08 J/g·°C"],1,"<b>0.520 J/g·°C.</b> Heat gained by water = (100.0)(4.184)(2.2) = 920 J = heat lost by metal = (25.0)(c)(70.8). c = 920/1770 = 0.520 J/g·°C. Each side uses its own ΔT."),
S("cal","2.00 kJ of heat raises a calorimeter's temperature by 0.80 °C. What is its heat capacity (calorimeter constant)?",["1.6 kJ/°C","2.5 kJ/°C","0.40 kJ/°C","2.8 kJ/°C"],1,"<b>2.5 kJ/°C.</b> C = q/ΔT = 2.00 kJ/0.80 °C = 2.5 kJ/°C. This number then converts any later temperature change into heat."),
S("cal","50.0 g of water at 80.0 °C is mixed with 50.0 g of water at 20.0 °C in an insulated cup. What is the final temperature?",["40.0 °C","50.0 °C","60.0 °C","100 °C"],1,"<b>50.0 °C.</b> Heat lost by hot = heat gained by cold: (50)(c)(80 − T) = (50)(c)(T − 20), so T = 50 °C. Equal masses of the same substance meet in the middle."),
I("cal","What type of calorimeter works at constant pressure and measures q<sub>p</sub> = ΔH?",["coffee cup","coffee cup calorimeter","coffeecup calorimeter","constant pressure calorimeter","constant-pressure calorimeter","coffee-cup calorimeter"],"Coffee-cup (constant-pressure) calorimeter","<b>Coffee-cup calorimeter.</b> Open to the atmosphere, so pressure is constant and the heat measured equals ΔH. It suits reactions in solution such as neutralization."),
// formation
C("form","Which has a standard enthalpy of formation of zero?",["H<sub>2</sub>O(l)","O<sub>3</sub>(g)","O<sub>2</sub>(g)","C(diamond)"],2,"<b>O<sub>2</sub>(g).</b> ΔH°<sub>f</sub> = 0 only for an element in its most stable form at 25 °C and 1 bar. Ozone and diamond are elements but not their standard forms (O<sub>2</sub> and graphite are)."),
C("form","The standard heat of formation of a compound refers to forming:",["1 g of the compound from its ions","1 mol of the compound from its elements in their standard states","1 mol of the compound from any reactants","The compound at 0 K"],1,"<b>1 mol from elements in their standard states.</b> That fixed reference makes the values combinable: any reaction's ΔH = ΣnΔH°<sub>f</sub>(products) − ΣmΔH°<sub>f</sub>(reactants)."),
S("form","Find ΔH° for 2SO<sub>2</sub>(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) → 2SO<sub>3</sub>(g). ΔH°<sub>f</sub>: SO<sub>2</sub> −296.9, SO<sub>3</sub> −395.2 kJ/mol.",["−98.3 kJ","−196.6 kJ","+196.6 kJ","−692.1 kJ"],1,"<b>−196.6 kJ.</b> 2(−395.2) − [2(−296.9) + 0] = −790.4 + 593.8 = −196.6 kJ. Multiply each ΔH°<sub>f</sub> by its coefficient; −98.3 kJ forgets the 2."),
S("form","Find ΔH° for CaCO<sub>3</sub>(s) → CaO(s) + CO<sub>2</sub>(g). ΔH°<sub>f</sub>: CaCO<sub>3</sub> −1207.1, CaO −635.5, CO<sub>2</sub> −393.5 kJ/mol.",["−178.1 kJ","+178.1 kJ","−2236.1 kJ","+1207.1 kJ"],1,"<b>+178.1 kJ.</b> (−635.5 − 393.5) − (−1207.1) = −1029.0 + 1207.1 = +178.1 kJ. Endothermic, which is why limestone must be heated strongly in a kiln."),
S("form","Using ΔH°<sub>f</sub> CH<sub>4</sub> −74.8, CO<sub>2</sub> −393.5, H<sub>2</sub>O(l) −285.8 kJ/mol, find ΔH° for CH<sub>4</sub> + 2O<sub>2</sub> → CO<sub>2</sub> + 2H<sub>2</sub>O(l).",["−604.5 kJ","−890.3 kJ","−1040 kJ","−754.1 kJ"],1,"<b>−890.3 kJ.</b> [−393.5 + 2(−285.8)] − (−74.8) = −965.1 + 74.8 = −890.3 kJ. Subtracting a negative reactant value makes the result less negative; sign errors there give −1040."),
S("form","Thermite: Fe<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>(s) + 2Al(s) → Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>(s) + 2Fe(s). ΔH°<sub>f</sub>: Fe<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> −822.2, Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> −1669.8 kJ/mol. ΔH°?",["−2492.0 kJ","−847.6 kJ","+847.6 kJ","−1669.8 kJ"],1,"<b>−847.6 kJ.</b> −1669.8 − (−822.2) = −847.6 kJ. Al and Fe are elements (zero). The huge release is why thermite welds rails."),
I("form","What is the enthalpy change for forming 1 mol of a compound from its elements in their standard states called?",["standard heat of formation","heat of formation","standard enthalpy of formation","enthalpy of formation","dhf","delta hf"],"Standard heat (enthalpy) of formation, ΔH°<sub>f</sub>","<b>Standard heat of formation, ΔH°<sub>f</sub>.</b> Elements in their standard states are the zero reference, so a table of ΔH°<sub>f</sub> lets you compute ΔH for any reaction."),
// hess
C("hess","Hess's law works because:",["Heat is a state function","Enthalpy is a state function, so ΔH is independent of the path","All reactions are reversible","Reactions occur in steps"],1,"<b>Enthalpy is a state function.</b> The ΔH from reactants to products is the same by any route, so you can add up any set of steps that starts and ends at the same place. Note that q itself is a path function; it is H that is a state function."),
C("hess","When a thermochemical equation is multiplied by ½, its ΔH:",["Is unchanged","Is halved","Is doubled","Changes sign"],1,"<b>Is halved.</b> ΔH is extensive: half the moles reacting means half the heat."),
S("hess","Given C(s) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) → CO<sub>2</sub>(g), ΔH = −393.5 kJ and CO(g) + ½O<sub>2</sub>(g) → CO<sub>2</sub>(g), ΔH = −283.0 kJ, find ΔH for C(s) + ½O<sub>2</sub>(g) → CO(g).",["−676.5 kJ","−110.5 kJ","+110.5 kJ","−393.5 kJ"],1,"<b>−110.5 kJ.</b> Keep the first equation; reverse the second (CO<sub>2</sub> → CO + ½O<sub>2</sub>, +283.0). Adding them cancels CO<sub>2</sub> and ½O<sub>2</sub>: −393.5 + 283.0 = −110.5 kJ. This ΔH can't be measured directly because burning carbon in limited O<sub>2</sub> always makes some CO<sub>2</sub>."),
S("hess","A → B, ΔH = +30 kJ and 2B → C, ΔH = −80 kJ. Find ΔH for 2A → C.",["−50 kJ","−20 kJ","+110 kJ","−110 kJ"],1,"<b>−20 kJ.</b> Double the first (2A → 2B, +60 kJ), then add the second: 60 + (−80) = −20 kJ. 2B cancels."),
S("hess","Given N<sub>2</sub> + O<sub>2</sub> → 2NO, ΔH = +180.6 kJ and 2NO + O<sub>2</sub> → 2NO<sub>2</sub>, ΔH = −113.1 kJ, find ΔH for N<sub>2</sub> + 2O<sub>2</sub> → 2NO<sub>2</sub>.",["+293.7 kJ","+67.5 kJ","−67.5 kJ","−293.7 kJ"],1,"<b>+67.5 kJ.</b> Add the two equations as written: 2NO cancels, giving N<sub>2</sub> + 2O<sub>2</sub> → 2NO<sub>2</sub>. ΔH = 180.6 − 113.1 = +67.5 kJ."),
I("hess","What is the law that says the ΔH of a reaction equals the sum of the ΔH values of the steps it can be divided into called?",["hess law","hesss law","hess's law","law of heat summation","law of constant heat summation","hess"],"Hess's law","<b>Hess's law</b> (law of heat summation). Reverse a step → flip its sign; scale a step → scale its ΔH; then add. It follows from H being a state function."),
// bond
C("bond","Breaking a chemical bond is always:",["Exothermic","Endothermic","Neither","Exothermic for single bonds only"],1,"<b>Endothermic.</b> Atoms in a bond are lower in energy than when separated, so pulling them apart requires energy input (ΔH &gt; 0). Forming bonds releases energy."),
C("bond","Why do bond-enthalpy estimates differ slightly from heat-of-formation values?",["Bond enthalpies ignore products","Tabulated bond enthalpies are averages over many compounds and assume the gas phase","Heat-of-formation data are always wrong","Bond enthalpies include entropy"],1,"<b>They are averages and gas-phase values.</b> A C–H bond is not identical in every molecule, and real reactants or products may be liquids. So propane gives −2054 kJ from bonds versus −2043 kJ from formation data."),
C("bond","The Born–Haber cycle is mainly used to calculate:",["Ionization energy","Lattice energy, which cannot be measured directly","Bond length","Entropy of ionic solids"],1,"<b>Lattice energy.</b> Every other step (sublimation, ionization, dissociation, electron affinity, ΔH°<sub>f</sub>) can be measured, and because H is a state function the one unknown, U, follows from the sum."),
S("bond","Estimate ΔH for H<sub>2</sub> + Cl<sub>2</sub> → 2HCl using H–H 436, Cl–Cl 243, H–Cl 431 kJ/mol.",["+248 kJ","−183 kJ","−431 kJ","+183 kJ"],1,"<b>−183 kJ.</b> Broken: 436 + 243 = 679 kJ. Formed: 2 × 431 = 862 kJ. ΔH = 679 − 862 = −183 kJ. Two H–Cl bonds form, so double 431."),
S("bond","Estimate ΔH for CH<sub>4</sub> + 2O<sub>2</sub> → CO<sub>2</sub> + 2H<sub>2</sub>O(g) using C–H 413, O=O 498, C=O 805, O–H 464 kJ/mol.",["−818 kJ","+818 kJ","−1166 kJ","−354 kJ"],0,"<b>−818 kJ.</b> Broken: 4(413) + 2(498) = 2648. Formed: 2(805) + 4(464) = 3466. ΔH = 2648 − 3466 = −818 kJ. Formation data give −802 kJ for water vapor; the gap reflects average bond values."),
S("bond","Find the lattice energy U of KCl: ΔH°<sub>f</sub> = −437, ΔH<sub>sub</sub>(K) = +89, IE(K) = +419, ΔH<sub>diss</sub>(½Cl<sub>2</sub>) = +122, EA(Cl) = −349 kJ.",["−718 kJ","+718 kJ","−1416 kJ","−156 kJ"],0,"<b>−718 kJ.</b> ΔH°<sub>f</sub> = ΔH<sub>sub</sub> + IE + ΔH<sub>diss</sub> + EA + U, so U = −437 − 89 − 419 − 122 + 349 = −718 kJ. Compare NaCl (−788 kJ): the larger K<sup>+</sup> ion gives a weaker lattice."),
I("bond","What is the energy released when gaseous ions combine to form 1 mol of an ionic solid, symbol U in the Born–Haber cycle, called?",["lattice energy","lattice enthalpy","u"],"Lattice energy","<b>Lattice energy.</b> It is large and negative (−788 kJ/mol for NaCl) and it is what pays for the uphill ionization step. It cannot be measured directly, so it is found from the Born–Haber cycle."),
// phase
C("phase","During boiling, heat is added but the temperature stays at 100 °C because:",["The thermometer is inaccurate","The heat goes into separating molecules (raising potential energy), not into kinetic energy","Water stops absorbing heat","Pressure falls"],1,"<b>The heat separates molecules.</b> Temperature measures average kinetic energy. At a phase change, energy goes into overcoming intermolecular forces instead, so T stays flat until all the liquid has vaporized."),
C("phase","Which relationship is correct?",["ΔH<sub>vap</sub> = ΔH<sub>sub</sub> + ΔH<sub>fus</sub>","ΔH<sub>sub</sub> = ΔH<sub>fus</sub> + ΔH<sub>vap</sub>","ΔH<sub>fus</sub> = ΔH<sub>sub</sub> + ΔH<sub>vap</sub>","ΔH<sub>sub</sub> = ΔH<sub>vap</sub> − ΔH<sub>fus</sub>"],1,"<b>ΔH<sub>sub</sub> = ΔH<sub>fus</sub> + ΔH<sub>vap</sub>.</b> Solid → gas directly gives the same ΔH as solid → liquid → gas (Hess's law)."),
C("phase","Which phase change is exothermic?",["Melting","Sublimation","Vaporization","Condensation"],3,"<b>Condensation.</b> Gas → liquid releases the heat that vaporization absorbed (ΔH<sub>cond</sub> = −ΔH<sub>vap</sub>). It's why steam burns are so severe."),
S("phase","How much heat is needed to melt 36.0 g of ice at 0 °C? ΔH<sub>fus</sub> = 6.01 kJ/mol.",["6.01 kJ","12.0 kJ","216 kJ","3.00 kJ"],1,"<b>12.0 kJ.</b> n = 36.0/18.02 = 2.00 mol. q = 2.00 × 6.01 = 12.0 kJ. No temperature term: melting happens at constant T."),
S("phase","A substance has ΔH<sub>fus</sub> = 9.95 kJ/mol and ΔH<sub>vap</sub> = 31.0 kJ/mol. What is ΔH<sub>sub</sub>?",["21.1 kJ/mol","41.0 kJ/mol","31.0 kJ/mol","3.12 kJ/mol"],1,"<b>41.0 kJ/mol.</b> ΔH<sub>sub</sub> = 9.95 + 31.0 = 40.95 ≈ 41.0 kJ/mol."),
S("phase","How much heat is needed to warm 50.0 g of water from 25.0 °C to 100.0 °C and then vaporize it all? (c = 4.184 J/g·°C, ΔH<sub>vap</sub> = 40.7 kJ/mol)",["15.7 kJ","113 kJ","129 kJ","2035 kJ"],2,"<b>129 kJ.</b> Warming: (50.0)(4.184)(75.0) = 15.7 kJ. Vaporizing: (50.0/18.02)(40.7) = 112.9 kJ. Total = 128.6 kJ. The phase change dominates; picking 15.7 or 113 means one segment was missed."),
I("phase","What is the direct change from solid to gas, whose enthalpy equals ΔH<sub>fus</sub> + ΔH<sub>vap</sub>, called?",["sublimation"],"Sublimation","<b>Sublimation.</b> Dry ice (solid CO<sub>2</sub>) sublimes at 195 K at 1 atm, which is why it leaves no puddle. The reverse, gas → solid, is deposition."),
