C("spont","A spontaneous process is one that:",["Happens quickly","Proceeds on its own without continuous outside input of energy","Always releases heat","Has ΔS<sub>sys</sub> &gt; 0"],1,"<b>Proceeds on its own.</b> It says nothing about speed: rusting is spontaneous but slow. Nor must it release heat (ice melting absorbs heat) or raise the system's own entropy (water freezing lowers it)."),
C("spont","The second law of thermodynamics states that in a spontaneous process:",["ΔE = 0","The entropy of the universe increases","The entropy of the system always increases","ΔH &lt; 0"],1,"<b>ΔS<sub>universe</sub> increases.</b> The system's entropy can fall if the surroundings gain more, as when water freezes below 0 °C and releases heat into the surroundings."),
C("spont","Why does a gas spontaneously spread into an evacuated flask but never gather back into one flask?",["Gas molecules repel","The spread-out state has vastly more possible arrangements (microstates), so it is overwhelmingly more probable","Pressure forces it","The process is exothermic"],1,"<b>More microstates.</b> With two flasks available, arrangements with molecules spread across both vastly outnumber those with all of them in one. The system ends up in the most probable state, the one with the highest entropy."),
C("spont","Water freezing at −5 °C is spontaneous even though ΔS<sub>sys</sub> &lt; 0. Why?",["ΔH &gt; 0","The heat released raises the surroundings' entropy (ΔS<sub>surr</sub> = −ΔH/T) by more than the system loses","Entropy doesn't apply to phase changes","It isn't actually spontaneous"],1,"<b>ΔS<sub>surr</sub> outweighs ΔS<sub>sys</sub>.</b> Freezing releases heat into cold surroundings, and ΔS<sub>surr</sub> = −ΔH/T is large when T is low. ΔS<sub>univ</sub> &gt; 0 below 0 °C; above 0 °C the balance tips the other way."),
C("spont","Diamond converting to graphite is spontaneous at room temperature, yet diamonds last for ages. This shows that:",["The process is actually nonspontaneous","Thermodynamics predicts direction, not rate","Graphite is less stable","Entropy decreases"],1,"<b>Thermodynamics predicts direction, not rate.</b> ΔG &lt; 0 means the conversion can happen, but a huge activation energy makes it immeasurably slow. Rate belongs to kinetics."),
S("spont","A reaction releases 92.4 kJ of heat at 298 K (ΔH = −92.4 kJ). What is ΔS<sub>surr</sub>?",["−310 J/K","+310 J/K","+0.310 J/K","−92.4 J/K"],1,"<b>+310 J/K.</b> ΔS<sub>surr</sub> = −ΔH<sub>sys</sub>/T = −(−92 400 J)/298 K = +310 J/K. Heat flowing into the surroundings increases their entropy."),
I("spont","What is the law that says the entropy of the universe increases in every spontaneous process called?",["second law","second law of thermodynamics","2nd law","2nd law of thermodynamics"],"Second law of thermodynamics","<b>Second law of thermodynamics.</b> It decides direction, where the first law only keeps count. ΔG &lt; 0 is the same statement written for the system alone."),
I("spont","What is the thermodynamic quantity that measures the randomness or disorder of a system (symbol S) called?",["entropy","s"],"Entropy","<b>Entropy, S.</b> More precisely, it reflects the number of microscopic arrangements available: S = k ln W. It increases with phase changes toward gas, higher temperature, more gas moles, and dissolution."),
// entropy & 3rd law
C("ent","Which process has ΔS &lt; 0?",["H<sub>2</sub>O(l) → H<sub>2</sub>O(g)","2H<sub>2</sub>O(l) → 2H<sub>2</sub>(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g)","N<sub>2</sub>(g) + 3H<sub>2</sub>(g) → 2NH<sub>3</sub>(g)","NaCl(s) → Na<sup>+</sup>(aq) + Cl<sup>−</sup>(aq)"],2,"<b>N<sub>2</sub> + 3H<sub>2</sub> → 2NH<sub>3</sub>.</b> Four moles of gas become two, so there are far fewer ways to arrange the particles. The other three make gas or dissolve a solid, which raises entropy."),
C("ent","Which has the highest standard molar entropy at 25 °C?",["H<sub>2</sub>O(s)","H<sub>2</sub>O(l)","H<sub>2</sub>O(g)","All equal"],2,"<b>H<sub>2</sub>O(g).</b> S<sub>solid</sub> &lt; S<sub>liquid</sub> ≪ S<sub>gas</sub>. Gas molecules move freely through a large volume, giving the most microstates (188.8 vs 69.9 J/mol·K for the liquid)."),
C("ent","The third law of thermodynamics states that:",["Energy is conserved","The entropy of a pure, perfect crystal is zero at 0 K","ΔS<sub>univ</sub> &gt; 0","ΔH°<sub>f</sub> of elements is zero"],1,"<b>A perfect crystal at 0 K has zero entropy.</b> There is only one possible arrangement (W = 1, S = k ln 1 = 0). This fixes a true zero, so tables list absolute entropies S°."),
C("ent","Why is S° of O<sub>2</sub>(g) at 25 °C not zero, even though its ΔH°<sub>f</sub> is zero?",["It's a data error","ΔH°<sub>f</sub> = 0 is a chosen reference, but entropy is absolute and only reaches zero for a perfect crystal at 0 K","O<sub>2</sub> is not an element","Entropy is always negative"],1,"<b>Different kinds of scale.</b> Enthalpies can only be measured as changes, so chemists choose elements as the zero. Entropy has a natural zero (third law); O<sub>2</sub> gas at 298 K has plenty of motion and arrangements, so S° = 205.0 J/mol·K."),
S("ent","ΔH<sub>fus</sub> of ice is 6.01 kJ/mol at 0 °C. What is ΔS<sub>fus</sub>?",["+22.0 J/mol·K","+6.01 J/mol·K","−22.0 J/mol·K","+2.20 × 10<sup>3</sup> J/mol·K"],0,"<b>+22.0 J/mol·K.</b> ΔS = ΔH/T = 6010 J/mol ÷ 273.15 K = 22.0 J/mol·K. Use kelvin; dividing by 0 °C would be undefined, which is a good reminder."),
S("ent","Find ΔS° for N<sub>2</sub>(g) + 3H<sub>2</sub>(g) → 2NH<sub>3</sub>(g). S°: N<sub>2</sub> 191.5, H<sub>2</sub> 130.6, NH<sub>3</sub> 192.5 J/mol·K.",["+198.3 J/K","−198.3 J/K","−129.6 J/K","+1.0 J/K"],1,"<b>−198.3 J/K.</b> 2(192.5) − [191.5 + 3(130.6)] = 385.0 − 583.3 = −198.3 J/K. Negative, as predicted from 4 → 2 mol of gas."),
S("ent","Benzene has ΔH<sub>vap</sub> = 30.8 kJ/mol and ΔS<sub>vap</sub> = 87.0 J/mol·K. Estimate its normal boiling point.",["354 K","0.354 K","2.82 K","273 K"],0,"<b>354 K (81 °C).</b> At the boiling point, liquid and vapor are at equilibrium, so ΔG = 0 and T = ΔH/ΔS = 30 800/87.0 = 354 K. Units: J over J/K."),
I("ent","What is the law that says the entropy of a perfect crystalline substance is zero at absolute zero called?",["third law","third law of thermodynamics","3rd law","3rd law of thermodynamics"],"Third law of thermodynamics","<b>Third law of thermodynamics.</b> It gives entropy an absolute zero point, which is why S° values in tables are absolute, unlike ΔH°<sub>f</sub>."),
// gibbs
C("gibbs","A reaction has ΔH &lt; 0 and ΔS &gt; 0. It is:",["Never spontaneous","Spontaneous at all temperatures","Spontaneous only at high T","Spontaneous only at low T"],1,"<b>Spontaneous at all temperatures.</b> ΔG = ΔH − TΔS = (negative) − (positive) is always negative. Both the energy and the disorder drives favor products."),
C("gibbs","A reaction has ΔH &gt; 0 and ΔS &gt; 0. It is spontaneous:",["At all temperatures","At no temperature","Only above T = ΔH/ΔS","Only below T = ΔH/ΔS"],2,"<b>Only above T = ΔH/ΔS.</b> At low T the positive ΔH dominates (ΔG &gt; 0); at high T the −TΔS term grows until it wins. Ice melting and limestone decomposition behave this way."),
C("gibbs","When ΔG = 0 for a process:",["It is spontaneous","It is nonspontaneous","The system is at equilibrium","ΔH = 0"],2,"<b>The system is at equilibrium.</b> There is no net drive in either direction, like ice and water at exactly 0 °C. Setting ΔG = 0 is how you get T = ΔH/ΔS."),
C("gibbs","Which statement about ΔG is <b>false</b>?",["ΔG &lt; 0 indicates a spontaneous process at constant T and P","ΔG is the maximum useful work obtainable","A large negative ΔG means the reaction is fast","ΔG depends on temperature"],2,"<b>\"A large negative ΔG means the reaction is fast\" is false.</b> ΔG indicates nothing about rate. A free-energy diagram can show products far downhill behind a high activation barrier."),
S("gibbs","For N<sub>2</sub> + 3H<sub>2</sub> → 2NH<sub>3</sub>, ΔH° = −92.4 kJ and ΔS° = −198.3 J/K. What is ΔG° at 298 K?",["−151.5 kJ","−33.3 kJ","+33.3 kJ","59 000 kJ"],1,"<b>−33.3 kJ.</b> ΔG = −92.4 − (298)(−0.1983) = −92.4 + 59.1 = −33.3 kJ. Spontaneous at 298 K. 59 000 kJ is what you get if you leave ΔS in J/K."),
S("gibbs","For CaCO<sub>3</sub> → CaO + CO<sub>2</sub>, ΔH° = +178.1 kJ and ΔS° = +160.5 J/K. Above what temperature is it spontaneous?",["1.11 K","298 K","1110 K","2856 K"],2,"<b>About 1110 K (837 °C).</b> Set ΔG = 0: T = ΔH/ΔS = 178 100 J ÷ 160.5 J/K = 1110 K. Above this, the entropy term wins. 1.11 K comes from mixing kJ with J/K."),
S("gibbs","For the same reaction (ΔH° = +178.1 kJ, ΔS° = +160.5 J/K), what is ΔG° at 1000 K?",["−17.6 kJ","+17.6 kJ","+130.3 kJ","+338.6 kJ"],1,"<b>+17.6 kJ.</b> ΔG = 178.1 − (1000)(0.1605) = 178.1 − 160.5 = +17.6 kJ. Still nonspontaneous, because 1000 K is below the 1110 K crossover."),
